/*
 * OCR B A-Level Physics (Advancing Physics, H557) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * Shape: { topicId: { name, questions: [{ q, marks, command, markScheme, diagram? }] } }
 * Topic ids unchanged so topics-config.js still matches.
 *
 * WHY THIS SHAPE. Every item is written as one part of a real structured
 * question, not as a revision flashcard: a stem (a device, an experiment, a
 * typed data table, a real situation) and then the demand. About half the items
 * are calculations, many multi-step or 'show that', as on the papers, and every
 * topic carries practical items (uncertainties, graphs, errors, improvements).
 * Multiple-choice items are not in this bank. Advancing Physics habits —
 * estimates, iterative models, real devices — are used throughout.
 *
 * TARIFFS. H557 parts are worth 1 to 6 marks, most 1–3, with 6* Level of
 * Response items. Mix per chapter group: 17% 1, 30% 2, 27% 3, 15% 4, 6% 5, 5% 6.
 *
 * MARK SCHEMES. OCR layout: marking points labelled C1 / A1 / M1 / B1, then
 * Allow / Not / ECF guidance; 6* items give indicative points by strand and
 * OCR's Level 3/2/1 descriptors.
 *
 * Data marked "written for this practice question" is invented but realistic and
 * every calculation has been checked.
 * Drawing canvas flag (diagram: true) enables the sketch area in practice.html.
 */

const PHYSICS_OCR_B_PRACTICE = {
  'B1': {
    name: 'Development of Practical Skills',
    questions: [
      { q: `A student builds a temperature sensor from a thermistor and a fixed resistor in a potential divider connected to a 5.0 V supply. The output voltage is read on a digital voltmeter. The display shows 2.37 V.\n\nState the resolution of the voltmeter.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `0.01 V  B1\nAllow ±0.01 V. Not 0.005 V (that is half the resolution).` },
      { q: `In an experiment to measure the Young modulus of a steel wire, a student uses a micrometer to measure the diameter of the wire at five places along its length (data written for this practice question).\n\nDiameter / mm: 0.36, 0.38, 0.37, 0.35, 0.37\n\nCalculate the uncertainty in the mean diameter, using half the spread of the readings.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(0.38 − 0.35) / 2 = ±0.015 mm (allow ±0.02 mm)  B1\nNot ±0.03 mm (full range).` },
      { q: `A student uses a Geiger–Müller tube and counter to investigate the decay of a short-lived radioactive source. Before bringing the source into the laboratory, the student records the count with no source present for 10 minutes.\n\nState why the student makes this measurement.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `To find the background count (rate) so that it can be subtracted from each reading / to correct the readings for background radiation  B1` },
      { q: `A student measures the speed of sound using a tuning fork held over a glass tube standing in water. The length of the air column above the water is adjusted until the first loud resonance is heard. The student calculates the wavelength as four times the measured length of the air column.\n\nSuggest why this method gives a value of the wavelength that is systematically too small.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `The antinode forms slightly above the open end of the tube, so the effective length of the vibrating air column is longer than the measured length / end correction is ignored  B1` },
      { q: `A student determines the Planck constant by finding the voltage at which each of several different coloured LEDs just begins to emit light. The wavelength of each LED is given by the manufacturer.\n\nIdentify the independent variable in this experiment.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `The wavelength (or frequency) of the light emitted by the LED / the colour of the LED  B1\nNot "voltage" (this is the dependent variable).` },
      { q: `A 470 μF capacitor is discharged through a 220 Ω resistor. A student wants to record how the potential difference across the capacitor changes with time during the discharge.\n\nSuggest why the student should use a data logger rather than a voltmeter and stopwatch.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `The time constant is about 0.1 s, so the discharge is too fast to take readings by hand; a data logger can take many readings per second  B1\nAllow: RC = 0.10 s so discharge complete in well under a second.` },
      { q: `A student investigates Boyle's law using a sealed syringe of air attached to a pressure sensor. After each push on the plunger the student waits about a minute before recording the pressure and volume.\n\nExplain why the student waits before taking each reading.`, marks: 1, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Compressing the gas does work on it and raises its temperature; waiting lets the gas return to room temperature so the temperature is kept constant  B1` },
      { q: `A student measures the refraction of a ray of light entering a semicircular glass block. The protractor reads to the nearest degree and the student estimates the uncertainty in each angle as ±1° (data written for this practice question).\n\nangle of incidence i = 40°\nangle of refraction r = 25°\n\nCalculate the percentage uncertainty in each angle and state which angle contributes the larger percentage uncertainty.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `i: 1/40 × 100 = 2.5%; r: 1/25 × 100 = 4%  B1\nAngle of refraction (r) has the larger percentage uncertainty  B1\nAllow ECF for the comparison from incorrect percentages.` },
      { q: `A student times 20 complete oscillations of a pendulum and records a total time of 32.4 s. The student estimates the uncertainty in the timing, due to reaction time, to be ±0.2 s.\n\nCalculate the period of the pendulum and its absolute uncertainty.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `T = 32.4 / 20 = 1.62 s  B1\nUncertainty = 0.2 / 20 = ±0.01 s  B1\nNote: ±0.2 s scores the first mark only.` },
      { q: `A thermistor is used in a potential divider as a temperature sensor. The output voltage is 3.10 V at 20 °C and 2.30 V at 40 °C. Assume the output changes linearly over this range (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the sensitivity of the sensor. Give a unit with your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `sensitivity = change in output / change in input = (3.10 − 2.30) / (40 − 20)  C1\n= 0.040 V K⁻¹ (or V °C⁻¹)  A1\nAllow −0.040 V K⁻¹; allow 40 mV K⁻¹.` },
      { q: `A temperature sensor has a sensitivity of 40 mV K⁻¹. Its output is displayed on a voltmeter with a resolution of 0.01 V.\n\nCalculate the resolution of the whole system in kelvin.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `resolution = resolution of voltmeter / sensitivity = 0.01 / 0.040  C1\n= 0.25 K  A1\nAllow 0.3 K. Note: 4 K (inverted ratio) scores 0.` },
      { q: `In an experiment to measure the Young modulus of copper, a student loads a copper wire clamped at one end and passing over a pulley. The student uses a wire about 2.5 m long rather than 0.5 m long.\n\nExplain why using a long wire improves the experiment.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `For the same stress, a longer wire gives a larger extension  B1\nso the percentage uncertainty in the measured extension is smaller (for the same ruler resolution)  B1` },
      { q: `A student measures the diameter of a wire with a micrometer and records 0.39 mm. Afterwards the student notices that the micrometer reads 0.02 mm when its jaws are closed with nothing between them. The diameter is used to calculate the Young modulus of the wire.\n\nExplain the effect of this error on the value of the Young modulus calculated by the student.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Zero error is a systematic error: the diameter recorded is too large (true value 0.37 mm), so the cross-sectional area calculated is too large  B1\nE = stress / strain = FL / Ax, so the Young modulus calculated is too small  B1` },
      { q: `A student places a GM tube near a radioactive source and records the number of counts in five separate 10 s intervals (data written for this practice question).\n\nCounts in 10 s: 212, 198, 225, 205, 190\n\nCalculate the mean count and its percentage uncertainty, using half the spread as the uncertainty.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mean = 1030 / 5 = 206 counts; uncertainty = (225 − 190)/2 = ±17.5 (≈ ±18)  B1\npercentage uncertainty = 17.5 / 206 × 100 = 8.5% (allow 8–9%)  B1` },
      { q: `A student uses a 512 Hz tuning fork and a resonance tube to measure the speed of sound in air. The student finds the wavelength to be 0.66 ± 0.02 m. The uncertainty in the frequency is negligible.\n\nCalculate the speed of sound and its absolute uncertainty.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `v = fλ = 512 × 0.66 = 338 m s⁻¹ (allow 340)  B1\n% uncertainty = 0.02/0.66 = 3.0%, so uncertainty = ±10 m s⁻¹  B1\nAllow 340 ± 10 m s⁻¹.` },
      { q: `A student determines the resistance of a length of constantan wire, as part of an experiment to measure its resistivity. The readings are (data written for this practice question):\n\np.d. = 6.0 ± 0.1 V\ncurrent = 0.250 ± 0.005 A\n\nCalculate the resistance of the wire and its percentage uncertainty.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `R = V / I = 6.0 / 0.250 = 24 Ω  B1\n% uncertainty = 1.7% + 2.0% = 3.7% (allow 4%)  B1` },
      { q: `A student measures the Young modulus of a steel wire. The measurements and their uncertainties are (data written for this practice question):\n\noriginal length = 2.500 ± 0.002 m\ndiameter = 0.37 ± 0.01 mm\nextension = 1.2 ± 0.1 mm\nload mass = 1.000 ± 0.005 kg\n\nIdentify the measurement that contributes the largest percentage uncertainty to the value of the Young modulus, and suggest one improvement to reduce it.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Extension (8.3%, compared with diameter 2.7% → 5.4% in area, length 0.08%, mass 0.5%)  B1\nAny one: use a larger load (still within the elastic limit) / use a longer wire / use a vernier scale or travelling microscope to measure extension  B1` },
      { q: `A student investigates how the pressure of a fixed mass of air in a flask varies with temperature. The flask is placed in a water bath and connected to a pressure gauge by a length of rubber tubing that is outside the bath. The student extrapolates a graph of pressure against temperature and obtains −250 °C for absolute zero.\n\nSuggest a reason why the student's value differs from −273 °C, and one change to the apparatus to reduce the difference.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Some of the air (in the tubing) is not at the temperature of the water bath / the flask is not fully submerged / the thermometer reading is not the air temperature  B1\nUse shorter (narrower) tubing / immerse the whole flask / stir the water and wait for thermal equilibrium  B1` },
      { q: `In an experiment to find the Planck constant, a student increases the p.d. across an LED until the student judges by eye that it has just started to glow. The laboratory lights are on during the experiment.\n\nSuggest how this affects the measured p.d., and one improvement to the method.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `A faint glow is hard to see in a bright room, so the p.d. recorded is too high (systematic error)  B1\nView the LED through an opaque tube / darken the room / use a light sensor / plot current against p.d. and extrapolate to find the threshold p.d.  B1` },
      { q: `A steel wire of length 2.40 m and diameter 0.38 mm hangs vertically. A load of mass 4.00 kg is added and the wire extends by 4.2 mm.\n\nShow that the Young modulus of steel is about 2 × 10¹¹ Pa.`, marks: 3, command: 'Show', markScheme: `A = π(0.19 × 10⁻³)² = 1.13 × 10⁻⁷ m²  C1\nstress = 4.00 × 9.81 / 1.13 × 10⁻⁷ = 3.46 × 10⁸ Pa and strain = 4.2 × 10⁻³ / 2.40 = 1.75 × 10⁻³  C1\nE = stress / strain = 1.98 × 10¹¹ Pa  A1\nNote: using the diameter as the radius gives 4.9 × 10¹⁰ Pa and scores 1 mark.` },
      { q: `In an experiment to measure the Young modulus of a steel wire, the measurements were:\n\nload = 39.2 N (uncertainty negligible)\nlength = 2.400 ± 0.005 m\ndiameter = 0.38 ± 0.01 mm\nextension = 4.2 ± 0.1 mm\n\nThe value calculated was 1.98 × 10¹¹ Pa.\n\nCalculate the absolute uncertainty in this value.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `% uncertainty in diameter = 2.6%, so in area = 5.3% (doubled)  C1\ntotal = 5.3% + 2.4% (extension) + 0.2% (length) = 7.9%  C1\nabsolute uncertainty = 0.079 × 1.98 × 10¹¹ = ±0.16 × 10¹¹ Pa (allow ±0.2 × 10¹¹ Pa)  A1\nNote: not doubling the diameter term gives 5.2%, ±1.0 × 10¹⁰ Pa: scores 1 mark.` },
      { q: `A student finds that an amber LED of wavelength 590 nm just begins to emit light when the p.d. across it is 2.10 V.\n\nCalculate a value for the Planck constant from these readings and the percentage difference from the accepted value of 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `eV = hc / λ, so h = eVλ / c  C1\nh = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 2.10 × 590 × 10⁻⁹ / 3.00 × 10⁸ = 6.61 × 10⁻³⁴ J s  A1\npercentage difference = 0.02/6.63 × 100 = 0.3%  B1\nAllow 0.3–0.4%; ECF from h.` },
      { q: `A thermistor and a 2.2 kΩ fixed resistor are connected in series across a 5.0 V supply of negligible internal resistance. The output is taken across the fixed resistor. The thermistor resistance is 1.5 kΩ at 30 °C and 1.0 kΩ at 40 °C.\n\nCalculate the output voltage at each temperature and the average sensitivity of the sensor over this range.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `V(30 °C) = 5.0 × 2.2/3.7 = 2.97 V  C1\nV(40 °C) = 5.0 × 2.2/3.2 = 3.44 V  C1\nsensitivity = (3.44 − 2.97)/10 = 0.047 V K⁻¹ (allow 0.046–0.047)  A1` },
      { q: `A charged capacitor discharges through a resistor. A data logger records the p.d. across the capacitor (data written for this practice question).\n\nt / s: 0, 10, 20, 30, 40\nV / V: 6.00, 3.81, 2.42, 1.53, 0.97\n\nThe values of ln(V / V) are 1.79, 1.34, 0.88, 0.43, −0.03.\n\nDetermine the time constant RC of the circuit.`, marks: 3, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `ln V = ln V₀ − t / RC, so a graph of ln V against t has gradient −1/RC  C1\ngradient = (−0.03 − 1.79)/40 = −0.0455 s⁻¹  C1\nRC = 1/0.0455 = 22 s  A1\nAllow 21–23 s.` },
      { q: `A student holds a 512 Hz tuning fork over a tube of air closed at the bottom by water. Resonance is heard when the length of the air column is 0.163 m and again at 0.497 m.\n\nCalculate the speed of sound in air and explain why this method removes the systematic error due to the end correction.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `difference in lengths = 0.334 m = λ/2, so λ = 0.668 m  C1\nv = fλ = 512 × 0.668 = 342 m s⁻¹  A1\nThe end correction is the same for both resonances so it cancels when the lengths are subtracted  B1` },
      { q: `A student uses a GM tube to measure the count rate from a protactinium-234 source, which has a half-life of about 70 s. To reduce the percentage uncertainty in each reading the student considers counting for 60 s instead of 10 s.\n\nExplain why counting for longer reduces the percentage uncertainty, and why it causes a different problem in this experiment.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Radioactive decay is random, so counts in equal intervals fluctuate  B1\nA longer count gives a larger number of counts, and the spread is a smaller fraction of the total (fractional uncertainty ≈ 1/√N falls)  B1\nBut the activity falls significantly during a 60 s count (almost one half-life), so the reading is not the count rate at a single time  B1` },
      { q: `A student measures the period T of a mass–spring oscillator for different masses m (data written for this practice question).\n\nm / kg: 0.10, 0.20, 0.40, 0.80\nT / s: 0.63, 0.89, 1.26, 1.78\n\nThe student plots lg(T / s) against lg(m / kg).\n\nlg m: −1.000, −0.699, −0.398, −0.097\nlg T: −0.201, −0.051, 0.100, 0.250\n\nDetermine the value of n in the relationship T = k mⁿ.`, marks: 3, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `lg T = n lg m + lg k, so gradient = n  C1\ngradient = (0.250 − (−0.201)) / (−0.097 − (−1.000)) = 0.451/0.903  C1\nn = 0.50  A1\nAllow 0.49–0.51.` },
      { q: `A student tests Boyle's law using a syringe connected to a pressure sensor. The volume scale reads to 0.5 cm³ (data written for this practice question).\n\np / kPa: 100, 125, 150, 200\nV / cm³: 30.0, 24.2, 20.1, 15.2\n\nThe student claims the data show that pV is constant. Deduce whether the data support this claim.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `pV values: 3000, 3025, 3015, 3040 (kPa cm³)  B1\nspread = ±20 about 3020, i.e. about ±0.7%  B1\nThe uncertainty in volume (e.g. 0.5/15.2 = 3%) is larger than the spread in pV, so the data are consistent with pV = constant  B1\nAllow comparison using half the scale division (0.25 cm³ → 1.6%).` },
      { q: `A student measures pairs of angles of incidence i and refraction r for light entering a glass block and plots a graph of sin i against sin r with error bars. The best-fit line through the origin has gradient 1.50. The steepest and shallowest lines that pass through all the error bars have gradients 1.56 and 1.45.\n\nDetermine the refractive index of the glass with its absolute uncertainty, and state whether the result is consistent with a manufacturer's value of 1.52.`, marks: 3, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `n = gradient = 1.50  B1\nuncertainty = (1.56 − 1.45)/2 = ±0.055 (≈ ±0.06)  B1\n1.52 lies within the range 1.44–1.56, so the result is consistent  B1\nAllow ECF on the uncertainty.` },
      { q: `A light-dependent resistor (LDR) is connected in series with a fixed resistor across a 6.0 V supply. The output voltage is taken across the fixed resistor. The resistance of the LDR is about 10 kΩ in dim light and 1 kΩ in bright light.\n\nExplain why the output voltage rises as the light gets brighter, and suggest a suitable value of fixed resistor to give a large change in output over this range.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Brighter light → LDR resistance falls → larger share of the supply p.d. across the fixed resistor (same current through both; V ∝ R)  B1\nso output voltage rises  B1\nA fixed resistor of about 3 kΩ (between 1 kΩ and 10 kΩ, e.g. √(1 × 10) kΩ) gives the largest change  B1\nAllow any value 2–5 kΩ with reason.` },
      { q: `A student finds the p.d. V at which four different LEDs just begin to emit light (data written for this practice question).\n\nλ / nm: 470, 525, 590, 630\n(1/λ) / 10⁶ m⁻¹: 2.128, 1.905, 1.695, 1.587\nV / V: 2.62, 2.38, 2.09, 1.98\n\nThe student plots V against 1/λ.\n\nDetermine a value for the Planck constant from these data.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `eV = hc/λ, so V = (hc/e)(1/λ) and gradient = hc/e  C1\ngradient = (2.62 − 1.98) / ((2.128 − 1.587) × 10⁶) = 1.18 × 10⁻⁶ V m (best-fit line gives 1.21 × 10⁻⁶)  C1\nh = gradient × e / c = 1.2 × 10⁻⁶ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ / 3.00 × 10⁸  C1\nh = 6.4 × 10⁻³⁴ J s  A1\nAllow 6.2–6.7 × 10⁻³⁴ J s. Note: using a single data point is not a gradient method — max 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Two students stand 50.0 m from a large wall. One claps so that each clap coincides with the echo of the previous one. The other times 20 intervals between claps and records 5.8 s. The students estimate the uncertainties as ±0.2 s in the time and ±0.5 m in the distance.\n\nCalculate the speed of sound with its absolute uncertainty, and identify which measurement is the larger source of uncertainty.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `time for one echo = 5.8/20 = 0.29 s; distance travelled = 2 × 50.0 = 100 m  C1\nv = 100/0.29 = 345 m s⁻¹  A1\n% uncertainty = 3.4% (time) + 1.0% (distance) = 4.4%, so ±15 m s⁻¹  B1\nTime (reaction time in judging coincidence) is the larger source  B1\nNote: using 50 m instead of 100 m (172 m s⁻¹) loses the first mark; ECF thereafter.` },
      { q: `A capacitor is discharged through a resistor of 47 kΩ ± 5%. The student finds that the p.d. across the capacitor halves in 15.3 ± 0.5 s.\n\nCalculate the capacitance and its absolute uncertainty.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `t½ = RC ln 2, so RC = 15.3/0.693 = 22.1 s  C1\nC = 22.1 / 47 × 10³ = 4.7 × 10⁻⁴ F (470 μF)  A1\n% uncertainty = 0.5/15.3 = 3.3%, plus 5% = 8.3%  C1\nC = 470 ± 40 μF  A1\nAllow ±39 μF.` },
      { q: `A GM tube is used to measure the decay of protactinium-234. The background count is 5 counts in 10 s. The count in 10 s is measured every 30 s (data written for this practice question).\n\nt / s: 0, 30, 60, 90, 120\ncount in 10 s: 205, 154, 115, 87, 66\n\nDetermine the half-life of protactinium-234.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `Corrected counts: 200, 149, 110, 82, 61  B1\nln(corrected count) against t is a straight line: values 5.30, 5.00, 4.70, 4.41, 4.11  C1\ngradient = −λ = (4.11 − 5.30)/120 = −0.0099 s⁻¹  C1\nt½ = ln 2 / λ = 70 s  A1\nAllow 68–72 s. Not subtracting background gives about 73 s: max 3 marks.` },
      { q: `A student wants to use a thermistor in a potential divider as an electronic thermometer for the range 0 °C to 100 °C. The output is connected to a voltmeter.\n\nDescribe how the student should calibrate the sensor and use the calibration to measure an unknown temperature.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Place the thermistor in a water bath with a reference thermometer (and stir)  B1\nRecord output voltage at a range of temperatures, e.g. every 10 °C from 0 °C (ice) to 100 °C (boiling), waiting for steady readings  B1\nPlot a calibration graph of output voltage against temperature  B1\nRead the unknown temperature from the graph using the measured output voltage (calibration curve need not be linear)  B1` },
      { q: `A student measures the time for 10 oscillations of a mass on a spring for different masses (data written for this practice question).\n\nm / kg: 0.10, 0.20, 0.30, 0.40, 0.50\ntime for 10 oscillations / s: 3.98, 5.60, 6.90, 7.93, 8.90\n\nThe period of the oscillator is given by T = 2π√(m/k).\n\nDetermine the spring constant k.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `T² = (4π²/k) m, so plot T² against m; gradient = 4π²/k  C1\nT² / s²: 0.158, 0.314, 0.476, 0.629, 0.792  C1\ngradient = (0.792 − 0.158)/0.40 = 1.58 s² kg⁻¹  C1\nk = 4π²/1.58 = 25 N m⁻¹  A1\nAllow 24–26 N m⁻¹.` },
      { q: `A copper wire of original length 2.00 m and diameter 0.30 ± 0.01 mm is loaded with increasing weights (data written for this practice question).\n\nload / N: 0, 10, 20, 30, 40\nextension / mm: 0, 2.4, 4.7, 7.1, 9.4\n\nDetermine the Young modulus of copper using the gradient of a graph of load against extension, and calculate the percentage uncertainty in your value due to the diameter alone.`, marks: 5, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `gradient of F against x = 40 / 9.4 × 10⁻³ = 4.26 × 10³ N m⁻¹  C1\nA = π(0.15 × 10⁻³)² = 7.07 × 10⁻⁸ m²  C1\nE = gradient × L / A = 4.26 × 10³ × 2.00 / 7.07 × 10⁻⁸  C1\nE = 1.2 × 10¹¹ Pa  A1\n% uncertainty from diameter = 2 × (0.01/0.30) × 100 = 6.7%  B1\nAllow 1.1–1.3 × 10¹¹ Pa.` },
      { q: `A flask of air in a water bath is heated and its pressure p is recorded at temperatures θ (data written for this practice question).\n\nθ / °C: 20, 40, 60, 80, 100\np / kPa: 107.2, 114.9, 121.8, 129.5, 136.4\n\nUse the data to determine a value for absolute zero in °C, and calculate the percentage difference from the accepted value of −273 °C.`, marks: 5, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `p is proportional to absolute temperature, so extrapolate the straight line of p against θ to p = 0  B1\ngradient = (136.4 − 107.2)/80 = 0.365 kPa °C⁻¹  C1\nintercept at θ = 0: 107.2 − 20 × 0.365 = 99.9 kPa  C1\nabsolute zero = −99.9/0.365 = −274 °C  A1\npercentage difference = 1/273 × 100 = 0.4%  B1\nAllow −270 to −276 °C; ECF for the percentage.` },
      { q: `A student is given a set of LEDs of known wavelengths, a variable power supply, a voltmeter, a milliammeter and a protective resistor.\n\nDescribe how the student could use this apparatus to determine a value for the Planck constant. Your answer should include how the data are analysed and how the uncertainty in the result could be reduced.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nMethod\n• LED in series with protective resistor; voltmeter across LED, milliammeter in series\n• increase p.d. slowly until the LED just emits light (or current just starts)\n• repeat for each LED of different wavelength; repeat readings and take means\nAnalysis\n• eV = hc/λ for the threshold p.d.\n• plot V against 1/λ; straight line of gradient hc/e\n• h = gradient × e / c\nUncertainty\n• view LED through an opaque tube / in a darkened room to judge the onset\n• or plot I–V characteristic and extrapolate the linear part to I = 0\n• use many LEDs over a wide range of wavelengths; error bars and worst-fit lines to find uncertainty in gradient\n• LED wavelengths have a spread, which limits the accuracy\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A student is asked to determine the capacitance of an unlabelled capacitor, thought to be about 1000 μF, by discharging it through a known resistor. The student has a 9.0 V supply, resistors, a switch, a voltmeter and a stopwatch, and may use a data logger.\n\nDescribe how the student could determine the capacitance. Your answer should include the measurements to be made, how the data are analysed, and the main sources of uncertainty.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nMeasurements\n• charge capacitor from supply, then switch to discharge through resistor R\n• choose R (e.g. 47 kΩ) so that RC ≈ 50 s, slow enough to time by hand; or use a data logger for small R\n• record p.d. at regular time intervals (e.g. every 10 s) until about 1/10 of the initial value; repeat\nAnalysis\n• V = V₀ e^(−t/RC)\n• plot ln V against t; straight line with gradient −1/RC\n• C = −1/(gradient × R); or find time to halve, t½ = RC ln 2\nUncertainty\n• tolerance of resistor (e.g. ±5%) — measure R with an ohmmeter\n• reaction time in reading voltmeter while it changes — use a data logger or video\n• voltmeter resistance not much larger than R provides a parallel discharge path (systematic)\n• worst-fit lines on ln V graph to find uncertainty in gradient\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` }
    ]
  },
  'B2': {
    name: 'Fundamental Data Analysis',
    questions: [
      { q: `A cable car is supported by a steel cable. The tension in the cable is 4.2 kN.\n\nState the SI base units of the newton.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `kg m s⁻²  B1\nAllow kg m/s²\nNot N, or units containing derived units such as J m⁻¹` },
      { q: `A smoothing capacitor in a power supply has a capacitance of 470 µF.\n\nWrite this capacitance in farads, in standard form.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `4.7 × 10⁻⁴ F  B1\nAllow 4.70 × 10⁻⁴ F\nNot 470 × 10⁻⁶ F (not standard form)` },
      { q: `A student measures the potential difference across a thermistor with a digital voltmeter. The display reads 1.37 V and the reading is steady.\n\nState the resolution of the voltmeter on this range.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `0.01 V  B1\nAllow ±0.01 V\nNot 0.005 V` },
      { q: `A student lists four quantities measured during an investigation of a trolley rolling down a ramp: speed, displacement, kinetic energy and time.\n\nIdentify the one quantity in this list that is a vector.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `displacement  B1\nNot more than one answer given` },
      { q: `The diameter of a gold nucleus is about 1.4 × 10⁻¹⁴ m. The diameter of a gold atom is about 2.9 × 10⁻¹⁰ m.\n\nState how many orders of magnitude larger the atom is than its nucleus.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `4  B1\n(ratio ≈ 2 × 10⁴)\nAllow 4 to 5\nNot 2 × 10⁴ with no order of magnitude stated` },
      { q: `Before measuring the diameter of a wire, a student closes the jaws of a micrometer with nothing between them. The micrometer reads 0.03 mm instead of 0.00 mm.\n\nState the name given to this type of error in the micrometer.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `zero error  B1\nAllow systematic error\nNot random error` },
      { q: `A student measures the period T of a simple pendulum for several lengths l. Theory predicts T = 2π√(l/g).\n\nThe student plots a graph of lg (T/s) against lg (l/m).\n\nState the value of the gradient of the graph predicted by the theory.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `0.5  B1\nAllow ½` },
      { q: `The Young modulus E of a material is given by E = stress / strain.\n\nShow that the SI base units of the Young modulus are kg m⁻¹ s⁻².`, marks: 2, command: 'Show', markScheme: `strain has no units, so E has the units of stress, N m⁻² (or Pa)  M1\nN = kg m s⁻², so kg m s⁻² × m⁻² = kg m⁻¹ s⁻²  A1\nNote: must clearly state strain is dimensionless (ratio of two lengths) for M1` },
      { q: `The speed v of a transverse wave on a stretched string is given by\nv = √(T / μ)\nwhere T is the tension in the string and μ is the mass per unit length of the string.\n\nShow that this equation is homogeneous with respect to units.`, marks: 2, command: 'Show', markScheme: `units of T / μ: kg m s⁻² ÷ kg m⁻¹  C1\n= m² s⁻², so √(T / μ) has units m s⁻¹, the same as v  A1` },
      { q: `A sprinter runs the 100 m in a race.\n\nEstimate the maximum kinetic energy of the sprinter. Show your reasoning.`, marks: 2, command: 'Estimate', markScheme: `sensible values stated: mass 50–100 kg and maximum speed 8–12 m s⁻¹  C1\nKE = ½ × 70 × 10² ≈ 4 × 10³ J  A1\nAllow any answer in the range 1.5 × 10³ J to 7 × 10³ J consistent with the values chosen` },
      { q: `In an experiment to determine the resistivity of constantan, a student measures the diameter of the wire with a micrometer as 0.38 ± 0.01 mm.\n\nCalculate the percentage uncertainty in the cross-sectional area of the wire.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `percentage uncertainty in diameter = 0.01 / 0.38 × 100 = 2.6 %  C1\npercentage uncertainty in area = 2 × 2.6 = 5.3 %  A1\nAllow 5 %\nNote: 2.6 % scores 1 mark` },
      { q: `A student times 10 oscillations of a mass on a spring. She repeats the measurement five times. Her results are shown below (data written for this practice question).\n\ntime for 10 oscillations / s: 15.2, 15.6, 15.3, 15.9, 15.5\n\nCalculate the mean time for 10 oscillations and its absolute uncertainty, using half the range as the uncertainty.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mean = 77.5 / 5 = 15.5 s  B1\nuncertainty = ½ × (15.9 − 15.2) = ± 0.35 s, so 15.5 ± 0.4 s  B1\nAllow ± 0.35 s` },
      { q: `A student measures the resistance of five samples cut from the same reel of wire, each of the same length. The results are (data written for this practice question):\n\nresistance / Ω: 4.71, 4.68, 4.73, 5.12, 4.70\n\nIdentify the outlier in these results and justify your choice.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `5.12 Ω  B1\nthe other four values lie within a spread of 0.05 Ω of each other; 5.12 Ω is about 0.4 Ω from their mean, many times the spread of the other readings  B1\nAllow any valid comparison with the spread of the other readings` },
      { q: `Two students each determine the acceleration of free fall g.\n\nStudent A obtains g = 9.62 ± 0.03 m s⁻².\nStudent B obtains g = 9.9 ± 0.4 m s⁻².\n\nThe accepted value is 9.81 m s⁻².\n\nCompare the two results in terms of precision and accuracy.`, marks: 2, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `A is more precise (smaller uncertainty) but its range 9.59–9.65 m s⁻² does not include 9.81 m s⁻², so A is not accurate / suggests a systematic error  B1\nB is less precise but its range 9.5–10.3 m s⁻² includes 9.81 m s⁻², so B is consistent with the accepted value / accurate  B1` },
      { q: `A cable car is pulled by a cable that makes an angle of 25° with the horizontal. The tension in the cable is 4.2 kN.\n\nCalculate the vertical component of the tension.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `vertical component = 4.2 × sin 25°  C1\n= 1.8 kN  A1\nAllow 1.77 kN, 1800 N\nNote: 3.8 kN (cos used) scores 0` },
      { q: `A student plots a graph of extension against load for a rubber cord. Each point has a vertical error bar. To find the uncertainty in the gradient, she draws a best-fit line and a worst acceptable line.\n\nExplain how she should draw the worst acceptable line and how she uses it to find the uncertainty in the gradient.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `worst line is the steepest (or shallowest) straight line that still passes through all the error bars  B1\nuncertainty in gradient = difference between the gradients of the best-fit and worst lines  B1\nAllow uncertainty = half the difference between steepest and shallowest lines` },
      { q: `A spreadsheet model of the discharge of a capacitor uses the iterative rule\nΔQ = −(Q / RC) Δt\nwith a time step Δt. The student finds that the model predicts a charge after 10 s that is noticeably lower than the value given by Q = Q₀e^(−t/RC).\n\nSuggest why the model gives a lower value, and how the student could make the model agree more closely.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `the model uses the charge at the start of each step for the whole step, but Q (and so the current) falls during the step, so too much charge is removed each step  B1\nuse a smaller time step Δt (more iterations)  B1` },
      { q: `A student measures the extension of a spring as 12 mm. She uses a metre rule with a resolution of 1 mm, reading the position of the pointer before and after loading. She takes the uncertainty in each reading as ± 0.5 mm.\n\nCalculate the percentage uncertainty in the extension.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `uncertainty in extension = 0.5 + 0.5 = ± 1 mm  C1\npercentage uncertainty = 1 / 12 × 100 = 8 %  A1\nAllow 8.3 %\nNote: 4.2 % scores 1 mark` },
      { q: `A student uses a hand-held stopwatch to time a pendulum. She estimates that her reaction time introduces an uncertainty of ± 0.2 s in each timing. The period of the pendulum is about 1.6 s.\n\nShe times 20 oscillations instead of one.\n\nCalculate the percentage uncertainty in the period when she times 20 oscillations.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `time for 20 oscillations ≈ 20 × 1.6 = 32 s  C1\npercentage uncertainty = 0.2 / 32 × 100 = 0.6 %  A1\nAllow 0.63 %\nNote: 12.5 % (one oscillation) scores 0` },
      { q: `An aircraft flies with an air speed of 220 m s⁻¹ heading due north. A wind blows from west to east at 35 m s⁻¹.\n\nCalculate the resultant velocity of the aircraft relative to the ground. Give its magnitude and its direction.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `magnitude = √(220² + 35²)  C1\n= 223 m s⁻¹  A1\ndirection: tan θ = 35 / 220, θ = 9.0° east of north  A1\nAllow bearing 009°\nAllow 220 m s⁻¹ (2 s.f.)` },
      { q: `A capacitor discharges through a 47 kΩ resistor. A student records the potential difference V across the capacitor at regular intervals and plots a graph of ln V (with V in volts) against time t in seconds. The graph is a straight line with gradient −0.0213 s⁻¹.\n\nCalculate the capacitance of the capacitor.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `V = V₀e^(−t/RC), so ln V = ln V₀ − t / RC and gradient = −1 / RC  M1\nRC = 1 / 0.0213 = 46.9 s  C1\nC = 46.9 / 47 000 = 1.0 × 10⁻³ F  A1\nAllow 1.0 mF, 1000 µF` },
      { q: `Copper is a metal in which the atoms are closely packed. The spacing between neighbouring copper atoms is about 2.5 × 10⁻¹⁰ m.\n\nEstimate the number of atoms in a 1.0 cm³ cube of copper.`, marks: 3, command: 'Estimate', markScheme: `side of cube = 1.0 × 10⁻² m  C1\nnumber of atoms along one edge = 1.0 × 10⁻² / 2.5 × 10⁻¹⁰ = 4 × 10⁷  C1\nnumber of atoms ≈ (4 × 10⁷)³ ≈ 6 × 10²²  A1\nAllow answers in the range 10²² to 10²³` },
      { q: `The table shows data for two moons of Jupiter.\n\nIo: orbital radius 4.22 × 10⁸ m, orbital period 1.769 days\nCallisto: orbital radius 1.883 × 10⁹ m, orbital period 16.69 days\n\nA student plots lg (T / days) against lg (r / m) for all of Jupiter's large moons and obtains a straight line.\n\nUse the data for Io and Callisto to show that the gradient of this line is about 1.5.`, marks: 3, command: 'Show', markScheme: `lg values: Io lg r = 8.625, lg T = 0.248; Callisto lg r = 9.275, lg T = 1.222  C1\ngradient = (1.222 − 0.248) / (9.275 − 8.625)  C1\n= 0.975 / 0.650 = 1.50  A1\nAllow use of lg (16.69 / 1.769) / lg (18.83 / 4.22)\nNote: answer must be given to at least 3 s.f.` },
      { q: `The speed v of waves on deep water is thought to depend only on the gravitational field strength g and the wavelength λ, so that\nv = k g^a λ^b\nwhere k is a dimensionless constant.\n\nUse units to deduce the values of a and b.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `m s⁻¹ = (m s⁻²)^a m^b  C1\npowers of s: −1 = −2a, so a = ½  A1\npowers of m: 1 = a + b, so b = ½  A1` },
      { q: `A student measures the corrected count rate from a radioactive source. At t = 0 the corrected count rate is 840 counts per minute. At t = 40 minutes it is 210 counts per minute (data written for this practice question).\n\nThe student plots ln (count rate) against time and obtains a straight line.\n\nDetermine the decay constant and the half-life of the source.`, marks: 3, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `gradient = (ln 210 − ln 840) / 40 = −ln 4 / 40  C1\nλ = 0.035 min⁻¹  A1\nhalf-life = ln 2 / λ = 20 min  A1\nAllow 5.8 × 10⁻⁴ s⁻¹, 1200 s\nECF from λ for half-life` },
      { q: `A student measures the intensity I of light from a small lamp at different distances d. At d = 0.20 m the light meter reads 160 lux. At d = 0.40 m it reads 41 lux (data written for this practice question).\n\nThe student suggests that I = k dⁿ.\n\nDeduce the value of n and state what this tells you about the lamp.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `n = lg (41 / 160) / lg (0.40 / 0.20)  C1\nn = −1.96, approximately −2  A1\nthe lamp behaves as a point source / the intensity follows an inverse square law  B1` },
      { q: `A red helium-neon laser has an output power of 5.0 mW and emits light of wavelength 633 nm.\n\nCalculate the number of photons emitted by the laser each second.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `E = hc / λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ / 633 × 10⁻⁹  C1\nE = 3.14 × 10⁻¹⁹ J  C1\nN = 5.0 × 10⁻³ / 3.14 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 1.6 × 10¹⁶ s⁻¹  A1` },
      { q: `A spreadsheet models the decay of a sample of 1000 unstable nuclei with decay constant λ = 0.10 s⁻¹, using the rule\nΔN = −λNΔt\nwith a time step Δt = 1.0 s.\n\nCalculate the number of nuclei remaining after 2.0 s according to the model, and compare it with the value predicted by N = N₀e^(−λt).`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `after 1 s: N = 1000 − 0.10 × 1000 × 1.0 = 900  C1\nafter 2 s: N = 900 − 90 = 810  A1\nN₀e^(−λt) = 1000e^(−0.20) = 819; the model is about 1 % too low (because it removes too many nuclei per step)  B1` },
      { q: `A student collects data on the power output of different sources of electromagnetic radiation (data written for this practice question):\n\nmobile phone 1 W; car headlamp 60 W; domestic microwave oven 900 W; local radio transmitter 5 × 10³ W; national TV transmitter 1 × 10⁶ W\n\nExplain why it is useful to plot these powers on a logarithmic scale, and state the spacing on the scale between the mobile phone and the TV transmitter.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `values span many (6) orders of magnitude  B1\non a linear scale all but the largest values would be crowded close to zero / could not be distinguished; a log scale gives equal spacing for each factor of 10  B1\nlg (10⁶ / 1) = 6, so they are 6 units (decades) apart  B1` },
      { q: `A student uses a resonance tube to measure the speed of sound in air at 20 °C. She obtains 338 ± 4 m s⁻¹. The value predicted for air at 20 °C is 343 m s⁻¹.\n\nEvaluate whether her result agrees with the prediction.`, marks: 3, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `percentage difference = (343 − 338) / 343 × 100 = 1.5 %  B1\nher range is 334 to 342 m s⁻¹, so the predicted value lies (just) outside her uncertainty range  B1\nso there is probably a systematic error, e.g. the end correction of the tube has been ignored / the temperature in the lab was not 20 °C  B1\nAllow conclusion that the result is close to agreement if supported by the range argument` },
      { q: `A student investigates a simple pendulum and plots a graph of T² against length l. Theory predicts T² = (4π² / g) l.\n\nHer best-fit line has gradient 4.03 s² m⁻¹. The steepest line through all the error bars has gradient 4.18 s² m⁻¹ and the shallowest has gradient 3.90 s² m⁻¹.\n\nDetermine a value for g with its absolute uncertainty, and comment on your value.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `g = 4π² / gradient = 4π² / 4.03  C1\ng = 9.80 m s⁻²  A1\nfrom the worst lines g ranges from 4π² / 4.18 = 9.44 to 4π² / 3.90 = 10.12 m s⁻², so uncertainty = ± 0.34 m s⁻² (± 0.3)  B1\nthe accepted value 9.81 m s⁻² lies within 9.8 ± 0.3 m s⁻², so the result agrees with the accepted value  B1\nAllow uncertainty from percentage uncertainty in gradient (about 3.5 %)` },
      { q: `A student determines the density of a steel ball bearing.\n\ndiameter d = 12.70 ± 0.02 mm (measured with a micrometer)\nmass m = 8.36 ± 0.01 g (measured with a balance)\n\nThe volume of a sphere is πd³ / 6.\n\nCalculate the density of the steel and its absolute uncertainty.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `V = π × (12.70 × 10⁻³)³ / 6 = 1.07 × 10⁻⁶ m³  C1\nρ = 8.36 × 10⁻³ / 1.07 × 10⁻⁶ = 7.79 × 10³ kg m⁻³  A1\npercentage uncertainty = 3 × (0.02 / 12.70 × 100) + 0.01 / 8.36 × 100 = 0.47 + 0.12 = 0.59 %  C1\nabsolute uncertainty = ± 46 kg m⁻³, so ρ = (7.79 ± 0.05) × 10³ kg m⁻³  A1\nAllow 7790 ± 50 kg m⁻³\nNote: omitting the factor of 3 for d³ loses the third mark` },
      { q: `A student loads a copper wire of original length 2.00 m and diameter 0.38 mm. Her results are shown below (data written for this practice question).\n\nload / N: 0, 10, 20, 30, 40\nextension / mm: 0, 1.45, 2.95, 4.40, 5.90\n\nThe wire obeys Hooke's law over this range.\n\nDetermine the Young modulus of copper from these data.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `gradient of load–extension = 40 / 5.90 × 10⁻³ = 6.8 × 10³ N m⁻¹  C1\ncross-sectional area = π × (0.19 × 10⁻³)² = 1.13 × 10⁻⁷ m²  C1\nE = (F / x) × L / A = 6.78 × 10³ × 2.00 / 1.13 × 10⁻⁷  C1\nE = 1.2 × 10¹¹ Pa  A1\nNote: using diameter instead of radius gives 3.0 × 10¹⁰ Pa and scores 2 marks\nAllow use of any pair of values from the table` },
      { q: `A student investigates how the period T of a mass–spring oscillator depends on the mass m. She expects T = k mⁿ, where k is a constant. She calculates logarithms of her results (data written for this practice question):\n\nlg (m / kg): −1.000, −0.699, −0.398, −0.097\nlg (T / s): −0.401, −0.250, −0.100, 0.051\n\nTheory gives T = 2π√(m / K), where K is the spring constant.\n\nDetermine n, and use the intercept of the lg T against lg m line to determine K.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `n = gradient = (0.051 − (−0.401)) / (−0.097 − (−1.000)) = 0.452 / 0.903  C1\nn = 0.50  A1\nintercept (lg m = 0): lg T = 0.051 + 0.50 × 0.097 = 0.0995, so 2π / √K = 10^0.0995 = 1.257  C1\nK = (2π / 1.257)² = 25 N m⁻¹  A1\nAllow 24–26 N m⁻¹` },
      { q: `A student drops a paper cake case of mass 1.2 g. A computer model treats the drag force as F = cv² with c = 6.0 × 10⁻³ kg m⁻¹ and predicts a terminal speed. Video analysis of the real cake case gives a terminal speed of 1.1 ± 0.1 m s⁻¹.\n\nShow that the model predicts a terminal speed of about 1.4 m s⁻¹, and evaluate the model in the light of the measurement.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `at terminal speed, weight = drag: mg = cv²  C1\nv = √(1.2 × 10⁻³ × 9.81 / 6.0 × 10⁻³) = 1.40 m s⁻¹  A1\nthe measured value range 1.0–1.2 m s⁻¹ does not include 1.40 m s⁻¹, so the model overestimates the terminal speed  B1\nsuggestion: c is too small for this case / drag may not be exactly proportional to v² / the case may tumble or not fall vertically  B1` },
      { q: `A capacitor of capacitance C is charged to a potential difference V₀ and then discharges through a resistor of resistance R.\n\nSketch two graphs: (i) V against time t, and (ii) ln V against t. On each graph, label the key features that would allow RC to be found.`, marks: 4, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `(i) exponential decay curve starting at V₀ on the V axis, tending to zero but not reaching it  B1\n(i) a feature labelled: e.g. V falls to 0.37V₀ (V₀/e) at t = RC, or constant-ratio property shown  B1\n(ii) straight line with negative gradient  B1\n(ii) intercept labelled ln V₀ and gradient labelled −1 / RC  B1` },
      { q: `A student uses a Geiger–Müller tube and counter to measure the decay of a sample of protactinium-234m. The background count is 12 counts per 10 s. Her results are shown below (data written for this practice question).\n\ntime / s: 0, 30, 60, 90, 120\ncounts in 10 s: 412, 309, 233, 176, 134\n\nShe expects the corrected count C to follow C = C₀e^(−λt).\n\nAnalyse the data to determine the half-life of protactinium-234m.`, marks: 5, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `subtract background: corrected counts 400, 297, 221, 164, 122  B1\nln C values: 5.99, 5.69, 5.40, 5.10, 4.80  B1\nln C against t is a straight line (constant decrease of about 0.30 every 30 s), confirming exponential decay  B1\nλ = −gradient = (5.99 − 4.80) / 120 = 9.9 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹  C1\nhalf-life = ln 2 / λ = 70 s  A1\nAllow 68–72 s\nNote: not correcting for background gives about 80 s and loses the first mark but ECF applies` },
      { q: `A student measures g by dropping a card of length 50.0 mm vertically through two light gates. The gates are 0.700 m apart. The card takes 35.2 ms to pass through the upper gate and 12.8 ms to pass through the lower gate. Each time is recorded to ± 0.1 ms.\n\nCalculate a value for g from these data and evaluate the experiment.`, marks: 5, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `v₁ = 0.0500 / 0.0352 = 1.42 m s⁻¹ and v₂ = 0.0500 / 0.0128 = 3.91 m s⁻¹  C1\ng = (v₂² − v₁²) / 2s = (15.26 − 2.02) / (2 × 0.700)  C1\ng = 9.46 m s⁻²  A1\npercentage uncertainty in v₂² is about 2 × 0.8 % = 1.6 %, so g ≈ 9.5 ± 0.2 m s⁻², which does not include 9.81 m s⁻² (difference about 3.6 %)  B1\nso a systematic error is likely, e.g. air resistance on the card / card not vertical so effective length is less than 50.0 mm / separation measured between wrong points on the gates  B1\nAllow any valid evaluation point supported by the numbers` },
      { q: `A student is investigating how the frequency f of the fundamental note from a stretched wire depends on the tension T in the wire. The length of the vibrating wire is kept constant. She does not know the relationship, but suspects it may be of the form f = kTⁿ.\n\nDescribe how she should collect data, and how she should analyse it graphically to find n and k and to decide whether her data support a relationship of this form.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n\nCollecting data\n• tension set by hanging masses over a pulley, T = mg\n• frequency found using a signal generator and vibration generator, adjusted to the largest amplitude of the fundamental, or a microphone and oscilloscope / frequency app\n• at least 6 tensions over a wide range (e.g. a factor of 5 or more)\n• repeat readings of f and use half the range as the uncertainty\n• keep length (and wire) constant\n\nAnalysis\n• take logs: lg f = n lg T + lg k\n• plot lg f against lg T\n• a straight line shows a power law\n• gradient = n; intercept on the lg f axis = lg k, so k = 10^intercept\n• curved line shows the relationship is not a simple power law (could then test ln f against T for an exponential)\n\nEvaluating the fit\n• add error bars from the uncertainty in lg f\n• draw best-fit and worst acceptable lines to find the uncertainty in n\n• compare n with the prediction n = 0.5 from v = √(T / μ) and f = v / 2L\n• check whether the prediction lies within the uncertainty range; identify outliers\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A student measures the temperature θ of a cup of hot coffee as it cools in a room at 20 °C. Her thermometer reads to ± 1 °C. Her results are (data written for this practice question):\n\ntime / min: 0, 5, 10, 15, 20\nθ / °C: 90, 72, 59, 49, 42\n\nA spreadsheet model predicts that the temperature excess (θ − 20 °C) falls exponentially with a constant k = 0.050 min⁻¹, so that the model predicts 45.8 °C at 20 minutes.\n\nEvaluate whether the student's data support the model. Your answer should include an analysis of the data.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n\nAnalysis of data\n• temperature excess: 70, 52, 39, 29, 22 °C\n• ln (θ − 20): 4.25, 3.95, 3.66, 3.37, 3.09\n• ln values fall by a nearly constant amount (0.28–0.30) every 5 min, so the graph of ln (θ − 20) against t is a straight line: cooling is exponential\n• or: ratio of successive excesses roughly constant (about 0.74–0.76)\n• gradient = (3.09 − 4.25) / 20 = −0.058 min⁻¹, so k ≈ 0.058 min⁻¹\n\nComparison with the model\n• the exponential form of the model is supported\n• but the measured k (0.058 min⁻¹) is about 15 % larger than the model's 0.050 min⁻¹\n• measured 42 °C at 20 min is lower than the predicted 45.8 °C; the difference (about 4 °C) is much larger than the ± 1 °C thermometer uncertainty, so the difference is real\n• the model underestimates the rate of cooling\n\nLimitations and improvements\n• evaporation from the open surface adds extra energy loss not in the model\n• the cooling constant depends on cup, surface area, draughts; the model's k may have been for a different situation\n• the model's time step may be too large if it is iterative\n• improve data: more readings, stirring, temperature sensor and data logger with finer resolution, repeat runs, lid to test evaporation\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` }
    ]
  },
  'B3': {
    name: 'Physics in Action',
    questions: [
      { q: `The lens in a smartphone camera module has a focal length of 4.0 mm.\n\nCalculate the power of the lens. Give the unit.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `P = 1/f = 1/(4.0 × 10⁻³) = 250 D  B1\nNote: unit (dioptre, D, or m⁻¹) required for the mark.\nNot 0.25 D (f left in mm).` },
      { q: `A student sets up a simple camera using a converging lens of focal length 5.0 cm. The object is 40 cm in front of the lens. The student uses the Cartesian convention, so the object distance u = −40 cm.\n\nShow that the screen must be placed about 5.7 cm from the lens to obtain a sharp image.`, marks: 3, command: 'Show', markScheme: `1/v = 1/u + 1/f  →  1/v = 1/(−40) + 1/5.0  C1\n1/v = −0.025 + 0.200 = 0.175 cm⁻¹  C1\nv = 5.71 cm (positive, so real image on far side of lens)  A1\nNote: answer must be given to at least 3 s.f. (5.71 cm) for the A1 in a "show that".\nNote: using 1/v = 1/40 + 1/5.0 gives 4.44 cm and scores 0 (sign error).` },
      { q: `A simple camera forms a sharp image of a 12 cm tall object. The object distance is u = −40 cm and the image distance is v = +5.7 cm.\n\nCalculate the height of the image on the screen.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `magnification m = v/u = 5.7/(−40) = −0.14 (magnitude 0.14)  C1\nimage height = 0.143 × 12 = 1.7 cm  A1\nAllow 1.7 cm with or without a negative sign (inverted image).\nNot 84 cm (ratio inverted).` },
      { q: `A simple model of the human eye treats the cornea and eye lens together as a single thin lens of power +60 D. A person reads a page held 25 cm from this lens.\n\nCalculate the distance from the lens to the retina for a sharp image of the page to form. Use the idea that the lens adds curvature to the wavefronts.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `curvature of wavefronts arriving at lens = 1/u = 1/(−0.25 m) = −4.0 D  C1\ncurvature leaving lens = −4.0 + 60 = +56 D  C1\nv = 1/56 = 0.018 m (1.8 cm)  A1\nAllow 1.79 cm.\nNote: 1/64 = 1.6 cm scores 1 mark (sign error in object curvature).` },
      { q: `A student measures the image distance v for a converging lens at several object distances u (data written for this practice question). The values use the Cartesian convention.\n\nu / m: −0.150, −0.200, −0.300, −0.500\nv / m: 0.302, 0.199, 0.151, 0.124\n\nThe student plots a graph of 1/v (y-axis) against 1/u (x-axis).\n\nDetermine the focal length of the lens, and state the gradient the graph should have if the lens equation is obeyed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `1/u values: −6.67, −5.00, −3.33, −2.00 m⁻¹ and 1/v values: 3.31, 5.03, 6.62, 8.06 m⁻¹  B1\n1/v = 1/u + 1/f, so the intercept on the 1/v axis (where 1/u = 0) = 1/f ≈ 10 D  C1\nf = 1/10 = 0.10 m  A1\nAllow intercept 9.9–10.2 D and f in range 0.098–0.101 m.\ngradient = +1 (straight line through points with slope 1)  B1\nAllow f found from each pair using 1/f = 1/v − 1/u then averaged, for C1 A1.` },
      { q: `A security camera has a lens of focal length 8.0 mm and a sensor whose pixels are 3.0 μm apart. It is used to read the number plates of cars 12 m from the lens. The characters on a UK number plate are 79 mm tall. The recognition software needs each character to be at least 10 pixels tall in the image.\n\nDetermine whether the camera can be used to read the number plates at this distance.`, marks: 5, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `1/v = 1/u + 1/f = 1/(−12 000 mm) + 1/8.0 mm  C1\nv = 8.005 mm (≈ 8.0 mm, image very close to the focal plane)  A1\nmagnification = v/u = 8.005/12 000 = 6.7 × 10⁻⁴  C1\nimage height of character = 79 mm × 6.7 × 10⁻⁴ = 0.053 mm (53 μm)  A1\nnumber of pixels = 53 μm / 3.0 μm ≈ 18, which is more than 10, so the plates can be read  B1\nAllow v ≈ f = 8.0 mm with a justification that u is much larger than f.\nECF from an incorrect image height for the final B1, provided a comparison with 10 pixels is made.` },
      { q: `A medical X-ray imaging system stores each pixel as a 10-bit binary number.\n\nCalculate the number of different grey levels each pixel can represent.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `N = 2ᵇ = 2¹⁰ = 1024 levels  B1` },
      { q: `A digital camera sensor has 4000 × 3000 pixels. Each pixel records red, green and blue intensities with 8 bits each. The image is stored without compression.\n\nCalculate the size of the image file in megabytes (1 byte = 8 bits; 1 MB = 10⁶ bytes).`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `number of pixels = 4000 × 3000 = 1.2 × 10⁷  C1\nbits = 1.2 × 10⁷ × 24 = 2.88 × 10⁸ bits  C1\nbytes = 2.88 × 10⁸ / 8 = 3.6 × 10⁷ bytes = 36 MB  A1\nNote: 12 MB scores 2 marks (8 bits per pixel used instead of 24).` },
      { q: `A satellite image of a square area of ground of side 2.0 km is stored as an array of 4096 × 4096 pixels.\n\nCalculate the resolution of the image in metres per pixel.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `resolution = 2000 m / 4096 pixels  C1\n= 0.49 m per pixel  A1\nAllow 0.488 m.\nNot 2.0 × 10³ / (4096 × 4096) (area divided by total pixel count).` },
      { q: `A 3 × 3 block of pixel values from a grey-scale image is shown below (each value 0–255). The centre pixel has been corrupted by noise.\n\nRow 1: 12, 14, 13\nRow 2: 15, 250, 14\nRow 3: 13, 12, 15\n\nA median filter replaces each pixel with the median of itself and its eight neighbours.\n\nDetermine the new value of the centre pixel and explain why a median filter is better than replacing the pixel with the mean of the nine values.`, marks: 3, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `values in order: 12, 12, 13, 13, 14, 14, 15, 15, 250 → median = 14  B1\nmean = 358/9 ≈ 40  B1\nthe mean is strongly affected by the single extreme (noisy) value, so the pixel stays much brighter than its neighbours; the median ignores the outlier and matches the surroundings, so the noise is removed  B1\nAllow "the mean only spreads the noise over the region / blurs it".` },
      { q: `In a grey-scale image of a faint galaxy, the pixel values only range from 80 to 140 on a 0–255 scale. A contrast-enhancement program stretches this range linearly so that 80 becomes 0 and 140 becomes 255.\n\nCalculate the new value of a pixel that originally had the value 110.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `new value = (110 − 80) / (140 − 80) × 255  C1\n= 127.5, so 127 or 128  A1\nAllow either 127 or 128.` },
      { q: `A data-logger must be able to code any one of 1000 different possible readings as a binary number.\n\nCalculate the minimum number of bits needed for each reading.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `b = log₂ 1000 = 9.97, so 10 bits (2¹⁰ = 1024 ≥ 1000)  B1\nNot 9 bits (2⁹ = 512).` },
      { q: `A rover on Mars sends back a colour image of 1024 × 1024 pixels with 24 bits per pixel. The radio link to Earth has a rate of transmission of 2.0 kbit s⁻¹ (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the time, in hours, to transmit one uncompressed image, and suggest one way to reduce this time without changing the radio link.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `bits = 1024 × 1024 × 24  C1\n= 2.5 × 10⁷ bits (25 165 824)  A1\ntime = 2.52 × 10⁷ / 2000 = 1.26 × 10⁴ s = 3.5 h  A1\nany one: compress the image (remove redundant information) / use fewer bits per pixel or fewer colour channels (e.g. grey-scale) / send fewer pixels (lower resolution or a cropped region)  B1` },
      { q: `Speech sent over a telephone line contains frequencies from 300 Hz to 3.4 kHz.\n\nState the bandwidth of this signal.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `bandwidth = 3400 − 300 = 3.1 kHz  B1` },
      { q: `A digital telephone system samples speech, which has frequencies up to 3.4 kHz. Each sample is coded with 8 bits. The system takes 8000 samples per second.\n\n(i) State the minimum sampling rate needed for speech of this frequency range.\n(ii) Calculate the rate of transmission of the digital signal.\n(iii) Explain what would happen to the reproduced speech if the sampling rate were reduced to 4000 samples per second.`, marks: 5, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(i) minimum rate = 2 × highest frequency = 6.8 kHz (6800 samples s⁻¹)  B1\n(ii) rate = samples per second × bits per sample = 8000 × 8  C1\n= 64 000 bit s⁻¹ (64 kbit s⁻¹)  A1\n(iii) 4000 Hz is less than twice the higher frequencies (components above 2 kHz)  B1\nthose components are sampled too rarely and are reconstructed as false lower frequencies (aliasing), so the speech is distorted / high-frequency detail is lost  B1` },
      { q: `An audio CD stores music sampled at 44.1 kHz with 16 bits per sample on each of two stereo channels.\n\nShow that the rate of transmission when the CD is played is about 1.4 Mbit s⁻¹.`, marks: 2, command: 'Show', markScheme: `rate = 44 100 × 16 × 2  C1\n= 1.41 × 10⁶ bit s⁻¹  A1\nNote: answer to at least 3 s.f. (1.41 Mbit s⁻¹) needed for A1.` },
      { q: `The output of a sensor is digitised. The signal has a total voltage range of 5.0 V and there is random noise of about 20 mV on the signal.\n\nCalculate the maximum number of bits per sample that it is useful to use.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `number of distinguishable levels = V_total / V_noise = 5.0 / 0.020 = 250  C1\nb = log₂ 250 = 7.97  C1\nabout 8 bits (more bits would give level spacings smaller than the noise, so add no information)  A1\nAllow 7 bits if justified (2⁸ = 256 levels gives a spacing of 19.5 mV, just less than the noise).` },
      { q: `A digital signal is sent along a long undersea cable. Every 50 km the signal passes through a repeater. The signal picks up noise as it travels.\n\nExplain why the digital signal can arrive almost free of noise, whereas an analogue signal sent the same way would not.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `each repeater only has to decide whether each pulse is a 0 or a 1 (two levels), so it regenerates clean pulses and the noise is removed  B1\nan analogue repeater amplifies the noise along with the signal, so noise accumulates over the whole distance  B1` },
      { q: `A television transmitter sends out radio waves from vertical aerial rods. A householder mounts a receiving aerial with its rods horizontal and finds that the received signal is very weak.\n\nSuggest why the signal is weak and how the reception could be improved.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `the radio waves are plane polarised with the electric field oscillating vertically  B1\nthe electric field only drives currents along rods parallel to it, so rotate the aerial so its rods are vertical  B1` },
      { q: `A smartphone battery is labelled 3000 mA h.\n\nCalculate the charge, in coulombs, that flows through the phone when the battery is fully discharged.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Q = It = 3.0 A × 3600 s  C1\n= 1.1 × 10⁴ C (10 800 C)  A1\nNote: 3000 × 3600 = 1.08 × 10⁷ C scores 1 mark (mA not converted).` },
      { q: `A resistor of resistance 470 Ω is used in a sensor circuit.\n\nCalculate its conductance.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `G = 1/R = 1/470 = 2.1 × 10⁻³ S  B1\nUnit (S or Ω⁻¹) required.` },
      { q: `Two resistors, of resistance 220 Ω and 330 Ω, are connected in parallel in a sensor circuit.\n\nCalculate the total conductance of the combination and hence its resistance.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `G = G₁ + G₂ = 1/220 + 1/330  C1\nG = 4.55 × 10⁻³ + 3.03 × 10⁻³ = 7.6 × 10⁻³ S  A1\nR = 1/G = 132 Ω  A1\nECF from incorrect G for the final mark.` },
      { q: `An LED in an indicator circuit must have 2.0 V across it and a current of 20 mA. It is connected in series with a resistor to a 9.0 V supply of negligible internal resistance.\n\nCalculate the resistance of the series resistor.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `pd across resistor = 9.0 − 2.0 = 7.0 V  C1\nR = V/I = 7.0 / 0.020 = 350 Ω  A1\nNote: 9.0/0.020 = 450 Ω scores 0.` },
      { q: `A temperature sensor is a potential divider made of an NTC thermistor in series with a fixed resistor of 10.0 kΩ, connected to a 5.0 V supply. The output voltage is measured across the thermistor. At a certain temperature the output is 1.5 V.\n\nCalculate the resistance of the thermistor at this temperature.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `pd across fixed resistor = 5.0 − 1.5 = 3.5 V  C1\ncurrent = 3.5 / 10 000 = 3.5 × 10⁻⁴ A or ratio R_T / 10.0 kΩ = 1.5 / 3.5  C1\nR_T = 1.5 / 3.5 × 10⁻⁴ = 4.3 kΩ  A1\nAllow 4.29 kΩ.` },
      { q: `A potential divider consists of an NTC thermistor in series with a fixed resistor connected to a 5.0 V supply. The output voltage is taken across the thermistor.\n\nExplain why the output voltage falls when the temperature of the thermistor rises.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `as temperature rises the number of free charge carriers in the semiconductor increases, so the thermistor's resistance falls (conductance rises)  B1\nthe pd divides in the ratio of the resistances, so the thermistor takes a smaller share of the 5.0 V and the output falls  B1` },
      { q: `A student builds a potential divider from two 1.0 MΩ resistors connected to a 6.0 V supply. She connects a digital voltmeter of resistance 10 MΩ across one of the resistors, expecting a reading of 3.0 V.\n\nCalculate the reading on the voltmeter.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `resistance of 1.0 MΩ in parallel with 10 MΩ = (1/1.0 + 1/10)⁻¹ = 0.91 MΩ  C1\nV = 6.0 × 0.91 / (0.91 + 1.0)  C1\n= 2.9 V  A1\nAllow 2.86 V.` },
      { q: `A thermometer uses a sensor whose output changes by 40 mV for each 1 °C change in temperature. The output is read by a voltmeter with a resolution of 0.01 V.\n\nCalculate the resolution of the thermometer in °C.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `resolution = smallest detectable change in output / sensitivity = 0.01 V / 0.040 V °C⁻¹  C1\n= 0.25 °C  A1` },
      { q: `A student investigates the emf ε and internal resistance r of a cell. She connects the cell to a variable resistor and an ammeter, with a voltmeter across the cell terminals. Her results are (data written for this practice question):\n\nI / A: 0.10, 0.20, 0.30, 0.40, 0.50\nV / V: 1.42, 1.34, 1.27, 1.19, 1.11\n\n(i) Use the data to determine ε and r.\n(ii) Suggest why she should open the switch between readings.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `(i) V = ε − Ir, so a graph of V against I has gradient −r and intercept ε  B1\nε = intercept = 1.50 V  B1\ngradient = (1.11 − 1.42)/(0.50 − 0.10) = −0.78 V A⁻¹, so r = 0.78 Ω  B1\nAllow r in the range 0.75–0.80 Ω; allow ε 1.49–1.51 V.\n(ii) a current flowing continuously heats the cell / runs it down, changing ε and r during the experiment  B1` },
      { q: `A car battery has an emf of 12.0 V and an internal resistance of 0.020 Ω. When the engine is started, the starter motor draws a current of 200 A from the battery.\n\n(i) Calculate the terminal pd of the battery while the starter motor is running.\n(ii) Calculate the power dissipated inside the battery.\n(iii) Explain why the headlights, connected in parallel with the starter motor, dim while the engine is being started.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(i) V = ε − Ir = 12.0 − 200 × 0.020  C1\n= 8.0 V  A1\n(ii) P = I²r = 200² × 0.020 = 800 W  B1\n(iii) the headlights are across the battery terminals, so the pd across them falls from about 12 V to 8.0 V because of the large "lost volts" in the internal resistance; less current and power in the lamps  B1` },
      { q: `A platinum resistance thermometer and an NTC thermistor are both used to measure temperature.\n\nExplain, in terms of charge carriers, why the resistance of the platinum increases slightly with temperature while the resistance of the thermistor falls sharply.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `in a metal the number density of free electrons is very large and does not change noticeably with temperature  B1\nincreased lattice vibrations scatter the electrons more, so resistance rises slightly  B1\nin the semiconductor the number density of charge carriers is small and increases rapidly with temperature as more electrons gain enough energy to be freed, so conductance rises and resistance falls sharply  B1` },
      { q: `A 20 m extension lead contains copper conductors, each of cross-sectional area 1.5 mm². The resistivity of copper is 1.7 × 10⁻⁸ Ω m.\n\nCalculate the resistance of one conductor.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `R = ρL/A = 1.7 × 10⁻⁸ × 20 / 1.5 × 10⁻⁶  C1\n= 0.23 Ω  A1\nNote: area left as 1.5 × 10⁻³ m² gives 2.3 × 10⁻⁴ Ω and scores 1 mark.` },
      { q: `A student measures the resistance R of different lengths L of a wire of diameter 0.32 mm. Her results are (data written for this practice question):\n\nL / m: 0.200, 0.400, 0.600, 0.800, 1.000\nR / Ω: 1.24, 2.43, 3.67, 4.86, 6.10\n\nDetermine the resistivity of the material of the wire and its conductivity.`, marks: 5, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `gradient of R against L, e.g. (6.10 − 1.24)/(1.000 − 0.200)  C1\ngradient = 6.1 Ω m⁻¹ (allow 6.0–6.1)  A1\nA = π(0.16 × 10⁻³)² = 8.04 × 10⁻⁸ m²  C1\nρ = gradient × A = 6.08 × 8.04 × 10⁻⁸ = 4.9 × 10⁻⁷ Ω m  A1\nconductivity σ = 1/ρ = 2.0 × 10⁶ S m⁻¹  B1\nNote: using the diameter instead of the radius gives ρ four times too large; scores max 3.\nECF for σ from an incorrect ρ.` },
      { q: `A steel wire of original length 2.00 m extends by 1.2 mm when a load is hung from it.\n\nCalculate the strain in the wire.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `strain = x/L = 1.2 × 10⁻³ / 2.00 = 6.0 × 10⁻⁴  B1\nNot a value with a unit.` },
      { q: `A student measures the Young modulus of steel piano wire. The wire is 2.50 m long and has a diameter of 0.40 mm. A load of 50 N produces an extension of 5.0 mm.\n\nShow that the Young modulus of the steel is about 2.0 × 10¹¹ Pa.`, marks: 3, command: 'Show', markScheme: `A = π(0.20 × 10⁻³)² = 1.26 × 10⁻⁷ m²  C1\nstress = 50 / 1.26 × 10⁻⁷ = 3.98 × 10⁸ Pa and strain = 5.0 × 10⁻³ / 2.50 = 2.0 × 10⁻³  C1\nE = stress / strain = 1.99 × 10¹¹ Pa  A1\nNote: answer to at least 3 s.f. required for A1.` },
      { q: `In an experiment to find the Young modulus of a wire, a student measures:\ndiameter = 0.40 ± 0.01 mm\nextension = 5.0 ± 0.1 mm\noriginal length = 2.500 ± 0.002 m\nThe uncertainty in the load is negligible. The value obtained is E = 2.0 × 10¹¹ Pa.\n\nDetermine the absolute uncertainty in E.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `percentage uncertainty in diameter = 2.5%, so in area = 2 × 2.5 = 5.0%  B1\npercentage uncertainty in extension = 2.0% (length 0.08%, negligible)  B1\ntotal percentage uncertainty in E ≈ 5.0 + 2.0 (+ 0.08) ≈ 7%  C1\nabsolute uncertainty = 0.07 × 2.0 × 10¹¹ ≈ 1.4 × 10¹⁰ Pa, so E = (2.0 ± 0.1) × 10¹¹ Pa  A1\nNote: not doubling the diameter uncertainty gives 4.6% and 9 × 10⁹ Pa; scores max 2.` },
      { q: `A student measures the diameter of a wire with a micrometer. When the jaws are closed with nothing between them, the micrometer reads 0.02 mm. The student does not correct for this.\n\nState the type of error this causes and explain its effect on the value of the Young modulus that the student calculates.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `systematic error (zero error)  B1\nevery diameter reading is too large, so the calculated cross-sectional area is too large, the stress too small and the value of E too small  B1` },
      { q: `A nylon fishing line has a diameter of 0.30 mm. The breaking stress of the nylon is 6.0 × 10⁸ Pa (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the largest mass that can hang at rest from the line without it breaking.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `A = π(0.15 × 10⁻³)² = 7.07 × 10⁻⁸ m²  C1\nF = σA = 6.0 × 10⁸ × 7.07 × 10⁻⁸ = 42 N  C1\nm = F/g = 42 / 9.81 = 4.3 kg  A1\nNote: using diameter as radius gives 17 kg; scores 1 mark.` },
      { q: `A climbing rope behaves like a spring that obeys Hooke's law up to a force of 2.0 kN, at which point its extension is 0.40 m.\n\nCalculate the elastic strain energy stored in the rope at this force.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `E = ½Fx = ½ × 2000 × 0.40  C1\n= 400 J  A1\nNote: Fx = 800 J scores 0.` },
      { q: `The cutting tip of a masonry drill bit is made of tungsten carbide, a ceramic. The tip must scratch and cut into brick without itself being dented or worn away.\n\nState the material property that the tip must have.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `hard (high hardness / resists indentation and scratching)  B1\nNot strong, stiff or tough.` },
      { q: `A glass rod can support a large load, but if its surface is scratched and the rod is bent, it snaps suddenly with no permanent bending beforehand.\n\nExplain this behaviour in terms of the microscopic structure of glass.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `glass is amorphous, with strong rigid (covalent/ionic) bonds and no planes along which dislocations can move, so it cannot deform plastically  B1\nthe scratch acts as a crack; the stress is concentrated at the sharp tip of the crack  B1\nthe high local stress breaks bonds at the tip, so the crack propagates rapidly through the material: brittle fracture  B1` },
      { q: `Pure copper is very ductile and can be drawn into thin wires. Brass, an alloy of copper and zinc, is harder and less ductile.\n\nExplain these properties in terms of the microscopic structure of the metals.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `copper consists of positive ions in a regular crystal lattice (in grains) held by a sea of free electrons  B1\nthe lattice contains dislocations, which can move through the crystal under shear stress so that planes of ions slip one row at a time, allowing plastic deformation at a relatively low stress (ductility)  B1\nin brass, zinc atoms of a different size distort the lattice  B1\nthe distortions pin / obstruct the movement of dislocations, so a larger stress is needed for plastic flow: brass is harder and less ductile  B1` },
      { q: `A student is asked to make an electronic thermometer for the range 0 °C to 100 °C using an NTC thermistor, a fixed resistor, a 5.0 V supply and a voltmeter.\n\nDescribe how the student should set up the circuit, calibrate the thermometer, and use the calibration to find its sensitivity and resolution.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nCircuit:\n• thermistor in series with the fixed resistor across the 5.0 V supply (potential divider)\n• voltmeter across the fixed resistor or the thermistor as the output\n• fixed resistor of similar value to the thermistor in mid-range, so the output varies over a large range\nCalibration:\n• thermistor (insulated / in a sealed tube) in a water bath with a liquid-in-glass or digital reference thermometer\n• heat water from 0 °C (ice and water) to 100 °C (boiling) in steps, stirring and waiting for equilibrium before reading\n• record output pd at each temperature; repeat with water cooling and average\n• safety: care with hot water / kettle; keep supply and meter away from water\nAnalysis:\n• plot a calibration graph of output pd against temperature; use it to convert readings to temperature\n• sensitivity = change in output / change in temperature = gradient of the graph (in V °C⁻¹); not constant because the graph is not linear\n• resolution = voltmeter resolution ÷ sensitivity; best where the graph is steepest\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A car designer uses three classes of material: a steel body panel (a metal), a porcelain spark-plug insulator (a ceramic) and rubber window seals (a polymer).\n\nExplain how the microscopic structure of each material accounts for its mechanical properties.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nMetal (steel):\n• crystalline lattice of positive ions in a sea of free electrons; non-directional metallic bonding\n• dislocations move under stress so planes slip: plastic deformation, ductile and tough (absorbs energy before fracture)\n• carbon atoms and grain boundaries obstruct dislocations, making steel stronger and harder than pure iron\nCeramic (porcelain):\n• strong directional ionic/covalent bonds in a rigid (partly crystalline, partly glassy) structure\n• dislocations cannot move, so no plastic flow: hard and stiff but brittle\n• cracks concentrate stress at their tips and propagate easily; strong in compression, weak in tension\nPolymer (rubber):\n• long tangled chain molecules with occasional cross-links\n• under stress, chains uncoil and straighten: large strain at low stress (low Young modulus)\n• cross-links pull the chains back, so the deformation is elastic; stiffens at large strain when chains are fully straightened\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` }
    ]
  },
  'B4': {
    name: 'Understanding Processes',
    questions: [
      { q: `A guitar string is plucked and a stationary wave is set up on it. A student uses a strobe lamp and measures the distance between two adjacent nodes on the string as 0.32 m.\n\nDetermine the wavelength of the progressive waves that superpose to form this stationary wave.`, marks: 1, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `λ = 2 × 0.32 = 0.64 m  B1\nNot 0.32 m or 0.16 m.` },
      { q: `A diffraction grating used in a school spectrometer is labelled "300 lines per mm".\n\nCalculate the spacing d between adjacent slits of the grating, in m.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `d = 1 / (3.00 × 10⁵) = 3.3 × 10⁻⁶ m  B1\nAllow 3.33 × 10⁻⁶ m. Not 3.3 × 10⁻³ (unit error in mm).` },
      { q: `A very dim light source is used in a Young's double-slit experiment so that only one photon at a time passes through the apparatus. A sensitive detector counts photons arriving at different points on the screen. In the 'explore all paths' model, a phasor is found for each path from the source to a point on the screen and the phasors are added.\n\nState how the resultant phasor at a point is related to the probability of a photon arriving at that point.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Probability is proportional to the (amplitude of the resultant phasor)² / square of the resultant phasor length  B1\nNot 'proportional to the resultant phasor' (square needed).` },
      { q: `Light travels through a sample of glass at a speed of 2.0 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹. The speed of light in a vacuum is 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.\n\nCalculate the absolute refractive index of the glass.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `n = c / v = 3.00 × 10⁸ / 2.0 × 10⁸ = 1.5  B1\nNo unit. Not 0.67.` },
      { q: `A tennis ball is struck horizontally from the top of a tall building and falls to the ground. Air resistance on the ball can be ignored.\n\nState the value of the horizontal component of the acceleration of the ball during its flight.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `0 / zero (m s⁻²)  B1\nNot 9.81 m s⁻².` },
      { q: `A blue LED emits photons each of energy 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.\n\nCalculate the energy of one of these photons in electronvolts (eV). e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ / 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 2.0 eV  B1` },
      { q: `A car of mass 1200 kg travels along a straight road at a constant velocity of 15 m s⁻¹.\n\nCalculate the momentum of the car. Give a unit with your answer.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `p = 1200 × 15 = 1.8 × 10⁴ kg m s⁻¹  B1\nAllow N s. Answer and unit both needed for the mark.` },
      { q: `Two loudspeakers are connected to the same signal generator and emit sound of wavelength 0.34 m in phase. A microphone is placed at point P. The path from one loudspeaker to P is 0.85 m longer than the path from the other loudspeaker to P.\n\nExplain whether the microphone at P detects a loud sound or a quiet sound.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Path difference = 0.85 / 0.34 = 2.5 λ  B1\nWaves arrive in antiphase / phase difference 180° (π rad), so they superpose destructively and the sound is quiet  B1\nAllow phasors at P point in opposite directions so resultant is (close to) zero.` },
      { q: `A clean zinc plate is placed on top of a negatively charged gold-leaf electroscope. When a bright visible-light lamp is shone on the plate, the leaf stays raised. When a weak ultraviolet lamp is shone on the plate, the leaf falls steadily.\n\nExplain these observations using the photon model of light.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Each electron absorbs the energy of one photon, E = hf; visible photons have energy less than the work function of zinc so no electrons are emitted however bright the lamp  B1\nUV photons have energy greater than the work function, so electrons are emitted and the plate/electroscope loses (negative) charge even when the UV is weak  B1\nNot 'UV has more energy' without reference to individual photons.` },
      { q: `In an electron diffraction tube, electrons are moving at a speed of 4.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ when they reach a thin graphite target. Relativistic effects can be ignored.\nh = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, mass of electron = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg\n\nCalculate the de Broglie wavelength of these electrons.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `λ = h / mv = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ / (9.11 × 10⁻³¹ × 4.0 × 10⁶)  C1\nλ = 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰ m  A1` },
      { q: `A student estimates the Planck constant using LEDs. For a red LED the student slowly increases the p.d. across it and records that it just begins to glow at 1.75 V. The manufacturer gives the wavelength of its light as 650 nm. (Data written for this practice question.)\ne = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹\n\nCalculate the value of the Planck constant given by this measurement.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `eV = hc / λ → h = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 1.75 × 650 × 10⁻⁹ / 3.00 × 10⁸  C1\nh = 6.1 × 10⁻³⁴ J s  A1\nAllow 6.07 × 10⁻³⁴ J s.` },
      { q: `In a Young's double-slit experiment with a laser, a student uses a metre rule to measure the distance across 10 fringe spacings on the screen. The reading is 32 mm ± 1 mm. (Data written for this practice question.)\n\nCalculate the fringe spacing and its percentage uncertainty.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `fringe spacing x = 32 / 10 = 3.2 mm  B1\npercentage uncertainty = (1 / 32) × 100 = 3.1 %  B1\nAllow 3 %. Note: percentage uncertainty is the same for one spacing as for ten.` },
      { q: `An aircraft is heading due north with an airspeed of 220 m s⁻¹. A wind blows towards the east at 40 m s⁻¹.\n\nCalculate the magnitude of the resultant velocity of the aircraft relative to the ground.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `v² = 220² + 40²  C1\nv = 224 m s⁻¹  A1\nAllow 220 m s⁻¹ (2 s.f.) only with working shown. Not 260 m s⁻¹ (scalar addition).` },
      { q: `A crate is dragged 12 m in a straight line across a horizontal floor by a rope. The tension in the rope is a constant 150 N and the rope makes an angle of 30° with the horizontal.\n\nCalculate the work done on the crate by the tension in the rope.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `W = Fx cos θ = 150 × 12 × cos 30°  C1\nW = 1.6 × 10³ J  A1\nAllow 1560 J. Note: 1800 J (no component) scores 0; 900 J (sin used) scores 1.` },
      { q: `A lift of total mass 650 kg is raised through a height of 24 m at a constant speed in 16 s by an electric motor. g = 9.81 m s⁻²\n\nCalculate the useful output power of the motor.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ΔE = mgΔh = 650 × 9.81 × 24 (= 1.53 × 10⁵ J)  C1\nP = 1.53 × 10⁵ / 16 = 9.6 × 10³ W  A1\nAllow 9.56 kW.` },
      { q: `A string is fixed at one end to a mechanical vibrator and passes over a pulley at the other end, where a mass hangs from it. At certain frequencies of the vibrator a stationary wave with clear nodes and antinodes is seen on the string.\n\nExplain how the stationary wave is formed.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Waves travel along the string and are reflected at the pulley (fixed end), so two waves of the same frequency (and amplitude) travel in opposite directions  B1\nThey superpose; at nodes they are always in antiphase and cancel, at antinodes always in phase and reinforce  B1\nAllow 'interfere' for superpose.` },
      { q: `A ball is thrown vertically upwards at speed u and is caught at the same height after a time T. Air resistance is negligible. Take upwards as positive.\n\nSketch a graph of velocity v against time t for the ball from the moment it leaves the hand until it is caught.`, marks: 2, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `Straight line with constant negative gradient (from +u at t = 0)  B1\nLine crosses the t-axis at T/2 and ends at −u at time T  B1\nNot a V-shaped graph (speed, not velocity).` },
      { q: `A tennis ball of mass 0.058 kg travelling horizontally at 20 m s⁻¹ is hit by a racket. It leaves the racket at 30 m s⁻¹ in the opposite direction. The ball is in contact with the racket for 5.0 ms.\n\nCalculate the mean force exerted by the racket on the ball.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Δp = 0.058 × (30 − (−20)) = 2.9 kg m s⁻¹  C1\nF = Δp / Δt = 2.9 / 5.0 × 10⁻³ = 580 N  A1\nNote: 116 N (velocities subtracted as 30 − 20) scores 0; ECF on Δp.` },
      { q: `A laser beam is shone through a double slit onto a screen 2.50 m away. The slit separation is 0.40 mm. The student measures the fringe spacing on the screen as 3.9 mm. (Data written for this practice question.)\n\nCalculate the wavelength of the laser light. Give your answer in nm.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `λ = xd / L  C1\nλ = 3.9 × 10⁻³ × 0.40 × 10⁻³ / 2.50  C1\nλ = 6.2 × 10⁻⁷ m = 620 nm  A1\nAllow 624 nm. Note: power-of-ten error scores max 2.` },
      { q: `Light of wavelength 589 nm from a sodium lamp is shone normally onto a diffraction grating with 600 lines per mm.\n\nCalculate the angle of the second-order maximum, and determine the highest order of maximum that can be observed.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `d = 1 / 6.00 × 10⁵ = 1.67 × 10⁻⁶ m and sin θ = 2 × 589 × 10⁻⁹ / 1.67 × 10⁻⁶ (= 0.707)  C1\nθ = 45°  A1\nd / λ = 2.8 so highest order is n = 2  B1\nNot n = 3 (sin θ cannot exceed 1).` },
      { q: `Ultraviolet radiation of wavelength 250 nm falls on a clean zinc surface. The work function of zinc is 4.3 eV.\nh = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹, e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C\n\nCalculate the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectrons in eV.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `E = hc / λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ / 250 × 10⁻⁹ = 7.96 × 10⁻¹⁹ J  C1\n= 4.97 eV  C1\nKE_max = 4.97 − 4.3 = 0.67 eV  A1\nAllow 0.7 eV. ECF from photon energy.` },
      { q: `In an electron diffraction tube, electrons are accelerated from rest through a potential difference of 3.0 kV. Relativistic effects can be ignored.\nh = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, mass of electron = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg\n\nShow that the de Broglie wavelength of the electrons is about 2.2 × 10⁻¹¹ m.`, marks: 3, command: 'Show', markScheme: `eV = ½mv² so ½mv² = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 3000 = 4.8 × 10⁻¹⁶ J  C1\np = √(2mE) = √(2 × 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ × 4.8 × 10⁻¹⁶) = 2.96 × 10⁻²³ kg m s⁻¹  C1\nλ = h / p = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ / 2.96 × 10⁻²³ = 2.24 × 10⁻¹¹ m  A1\nAnswer must be given to at least 3 s.f. for the A1. Allow v = 3.25 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹ route.` },
      { q: `A telecommunications optical fibre is 25 km long. The refractive index of the glass core is 1.48. c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹\n\nCalculate the time taken for a pulse of light travelling along the axis of the fibre to pass from one end to the other.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `v = c / n = 3.00 × 10⁸ / 1.48  C1\nv = 2.03 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and t = 25 × 10³ / 2.03 × 10⁸  C1\nt = 1.2 × 10⁻⁴ s  A1\nAllow 123 μs. Note: 8.3 × 10⁻⁵ s (speed in vacuum used) scores 0; 5.6 × 10⁻⁵ s (c multiplied by n) scores 0.` },
      { q: `A microwave transmitter faces a flat metal plate. A stationary wave forms between them. A student moves a microwave probe along the line between transmitter and plate and finds positions of minimum signal. The distance from the first minimum to the eleventh minimum is 14.2 cm. (Data written for this practice question.) c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹\n\nDetermine the frequency of the microwaves.`, marks: 3, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `10 node spacings = 14.2 cm, so λ/2 = 1.42 cm  C1\nλ = 2.84 cm = 0.0284 m  C1\nf = c / λ = 3.00 × 10⁸ / 0.0284 = 1.1 × 10¹⁰ Hz  A1\nAllow 1.06 × 10¹⁰ Hz. Note: using 11 spacings (λ = 2.58 cm, f = 1.2 × 10¹⁰ Hz) scores max 2.` },
      { q: `A stone is thrown horizontally at 12 m s⁻¹ from the top of a vertical cliff 20.0 m high and lands on the flat beach below. Air resistance is negligible. g = 9.81 m s⁻²\n\nCalculate the horizontal distance from the foot of the cliff to where the stone lands.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Vertical motion: 20.0 = ½ × 9.81 × t²  C1\nt = 2.02 s  C1\nHorizontal distance = 12 × 2.02 = 24 m  A1\nAllow 24.2 m. ECF on t.` },
      { q: `A student uses a spreadsheet to model an object falling from rest with no air resistance. The model uses time steps Δt = 0.10 s and, for each step, calculates:\nnew velocity = old velocity + gΔt\nnew displacement = old displacement + (new velocity × Δt)\nwith g = 9.81 m s⁻².\n\nCalculate the displacement predicted by the model after 0.20 s and compare it with the value given by the equations of motion for uniform acceleration.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `After step 1: v = 0.981 m s⁻¹, s = 0.0981 m  B1\nAfter step 2: v = 1.962 m s⁻¹, s = 0.0981 + 0.1962 = 0.294 m  B1\ns = ½gt² = 0.196 m, so the model overestimates (because each step uses the velocity at the end of the interval); smaller Δt would reduce the discrepancy  B1` },
      { q: `On a level air track, glider A of mass 0.80 kg moves at 0.60 m s⁻¹ towards a stationary glider B of mass 0.40 kg. The gliders collide and stick together.\n\nCalculate the velocity of the gliders after the collision and show that the collision is inelastic.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `0.80 × 0.60 = (0.80 + 0.40) × v  C1\nv = 0.40 m s⁻¹ (in the original direction of A)  A1\nKE before = ½ × 0.80 × 0.60² = 0.144 J; KE after = ½ × 1.20 × 0.40² = 0.096 J, so kinetic energy is not conserved  B1\nECF on v for the B1.` },
      { q: `Single photons of red light pass one at a time through two narrow slits towards a screen. At point Q on the screen, the path from the source through one slit is exactly 1.5 wavelengths longer than the path through the other slit.\n\nExplain, using the 'explore all paths' phasor model, why photons are (almost) never detected at Q.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The phasor for each path rotates through one full turn (2π) for every wavelength travelled / at frequency f = E/h  B1\nThe extra 1.5λ means the two phasors arriving at Q point in opposite directions / differ by half a turn  B1\nResultant phasor amplitude is zero, and probability ∝ (resultant amplitude)², so probability of arrival at Q is zero  B1` },
      { q: `A student shines a ray of light into a rectangular glass block and measures the angle of incidence i in air and the angle of refraction r in the glass. (Data written for this practice question.)\n\ni / °: 20.0, 30.0, 40.0, 50.0, 60.0\nr / °: 13.0, 19.5, 25.5, 30.5, 35.5\n\nUse the data to determine the refractive index of the glass, and explain why plotting a graph of sin i against sin r gives a more reliable value than using a single pair of readings.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `Values of sin i (0.342, 0.500, 0.643, 0.766, 0.866) and sin r (0.225, 0.334, 0.431, 0.508, 0.581) calculated  B1\nn = sin i / sin r or gradient of sin i against sin r used  M1\nn = 1.50  A1\nAllow 1.49–1.52.\nGraph: best-fit line averages out random errors in individual angle readings / anomalous points can be identified / a line through the origin confirms n = sin i / sin r  B1` },
      { q: `In a photoelectric experiment, monochromatic light of different frequencies f falls on a metal surface and the maximum kinetic energy KE_max of the emitted electrons is measured. (Data written for this practice question.)\n\nf / 10¹⁴ Hz: 6.0, 7.0, 8.0, 9.0, 10.0\nKE_max / eV: 0.19, 0.60, 1.02, 1.43, 1.84\n\ne = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C\n\nUse the data to determine a value for the Planck constant h and the work function φ of the metal in eV.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `hf = φ + KE_max, so gradient of KE_max against f = h  M1\ngradient = (1.84 − 0.19) / (4.0 × 10¹⁴) = 4.1 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV s; h = 4.1 × 10⁻¹⁵ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J s  A1\nThreshold frequency / intercept: f₀ = 5.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz or KE-axis intercept = −2.3 eV  M1\nφ = 2.3 eV  A1\nAllow h in range 6.5–6.7 × 10⁻³⁴ J s; φ in range 2.2–2.4 eV. ECF on gradient for φ.` },
      { q: `An electron diffraction tube produces a pattern of bright concentric rings on a fluorescent screen when electrons pass through a thin layer of polycrystalline graphite. The accelerating p.d. is increased from 2.0 kV to 4.0 kV.\n\nExplain why the rings are evidence that electrons behave as waves, and predict, with a calculation, how the radius of the rings changes.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Rings are a diffraction/interference pattern: electron waves diffracted by planes of atoms superpose, so electrons arrive with high probability only where waves/phasors add in phase  B1\nThe spacing of atoms is similar to the de Broglie wavelength, so diffraction is significant  B1\nλ = h / √(2meV), so λ ∝ 1/√V and λ decreases by a factor of √2 (to 0.71 of its value)  M1\nAngles of diffraction are smaller, so ring radius decreases to about 0.71 of its original value  A1` },
      { q: `A car of mass 1100 kg travelling at 22 m s⁻¹ brakes with a constant deceleration and comes to rest in a distance of 40 m.\n\nCalculate the mean braking force, and show that the work done by this force is equal to the kinetic energy lost by the car.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `0 = 22² − 2 × a × 40  C1\na = 6.05 m s⁻²  A1\nF = ma = 1100 × 6.05 = 6.7 × 10³ N  A1\nWork done = 6655 × 40 = 2.66 × 10⁵ J and ½mv² = ½ × 1100 × 22² = 2.66 × 10⁵ J  B1\nECF on a.` },
      { q: `A student determines g using a card of length 5.0 cm fixed to a falling mass. The card falls vertically through two light gates A and B, 0.500 m apart, connected to a data logger. The card takes 25.0 ms to pass through A and 13.5 ms to pass through B. (Data written for this practice question.)\n\nCalculate a value of g from these data, and suggest one source of systematic error in the method together with how it could be reduced.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `v_A = 0.050 / 0.0250 = 2.00 m s⁻¹ and v_B = 0.050 / 0.0135 = 3.70 m s⁻¹  C1\ng = (v_B² − v_A²) / 2s = (13.7 − 4.00) / (2 × 0.500)  C1\ng = 9.7 m s⁻²  A1\nAllow 9.72 m s⁻².\nSystematic error: air resistance on the card/mass (use a denser/heavier mass or narrower card) / card not falling vertically through the beam (use a guide) / speed measured is mean speed in the gate, not at its centre (use shorter card or wider spacing)  B1` },
      { q: `A double slit and a diffraction grating with the same slit spacing are each illuminated in turn by the same laser. Both produce bright maxima at the same angles, but the maxima from the grating are much narrower and brighter.\n\nExplain this difference using phasors.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `At a maximum, path difference between neighbouring slits is nλ, so phasors from all slits point in the same direction  B1\nWith many slits (N) the resultant amplitude is N times one phasor, so intensity is much greater than with two  B1\nAt a small angle away from the maximum, phasors from the many slits each differ by a small angle and curl round into a closed loop, so the resultant falls to (almost) zero very quickly — maxima are sharp  B1\nWith only two slits the resultant changes gradually with angle (two phasors only cancel at half a turn), so fringes are broad  B1` },
      { q: `A golf ball is struck from level ground with a velocity of 25 m s⁻¹ at 35° above the horizontal. Air resistance is negligible. g = 9.81 m s⁻²\n\nCalculate the maximum height reached by the ball and the horizontal distance it travels before landing on the ground.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Components: v_h = 25 cos 35° = 20.5 m s⁻¹ and v_v = 25 sin 35° = 14.3 m s⁻¹  B1\n0 = 14.3² − 2 × 9.81 × h  C1\nh = 10 m (10.5 m)  A1\nTime of flight t = 2 × 14.3 / 9.81 = 2.92 s  C1\nRange = 20.5 × 2.92 = 60 m  A1\nAllow 59.9 m. ECF on components and t.` },
      { q: `A string of length 0.900 m between a vibrator and a pulley has stationary waves with 1, 2, 3 and 4 loops at the following frequencies. (Data written for this practice question.)\n\nnumber of loops: 1, 2, 3, 4\nfrequency / Hz: 26, 51, 77, 103\n\nDetermine the speed of the waves on the string, with an estimate of its absolute uncertainty.`, marks: 5, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `λ = 2L / n, e.g. λ = 1.80 m for one loop, 0.450 m for four loops  B1\nv = fλ for each row: 46.8, 45.9, 46.2, 46.35 m s⁻¹  C1\nMean v = 46.3 m s⁻¹  A1\nAllow 46 m s⁻¹. Allow gradient of f against n = v / 2L used.\nUncertainty from half the range: (46.8 − 45.9) / 2  C1\n= ± 0.5 m s⁻¹ (approximately)  A1` },
      { q: `A student is asked to determine the wavelength of light from a laser as accurately as possible using a double slit of known slit separation, about 0.5 mm.\n\nDescribe how the student should carry out the experiment and analyse the results to obtain an accurate value of the wavelength. Include how uncertainties are reduced and any safety precautions.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nMethod and measurements\n• laser shone through double slit onto a screen/wall a few metres away\n• measure slit-to-screen distance L with a tape measure\n• measure across several (e.g. 10) fringe spacings with a metre rule or vernier calipers and divide\n• slit separation d measured with a travelling microscope or taken from the manufacturer's value\nAnalysis\n• λ = xd / L\n• repeat for several values of L and plot x against L; gradient = λ / d, so λ = gradient × d\n• line should pass through the origin (checks for systematic error)\nUncertainties and safety\n• measuring many fringes reduces percentage uncertainty in x; large L gives larger x\n• darkened room makes fringe centres easier to judge\n• do not look into the beam or its reflections; class 2 laser; remove reflective surfaces; warning signs\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A science magazine article claims: "Light is a wave and electrons are particles." A physicist replies that both light and electrons show quantum behaviour: they are emitted and detected as quanta, but where they arrive is predicted by adding phasors for all possible paths.\n\nEvaluate the physicist's reply, using evidence from the photoelectric effect, electron diffraction and the 'explore all paths' model.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nLight as photons (photoelectric effect)\n• emission occurs only above a threshold frequency, whatever the intensity\n• each electron absorbs one photon of energy E = hf; hf = φ + KE_max\n• KE_max depends on frequency, not intensity; intensity changes the rate of emission\n• wave model cannot explain the threshold or instant emission\nElectrons as waves (electron diffraction)\n• electrons passing through graphite produce diffraction rings\n• de Broglie wavelength λ = h / p is similar to atomic spacing\n• increasing accelerating p.d. reduces λ and the ring size, as predicted\n'Explore all paths' model\n• a quantum is detected at one place, but the probability is found by adding phasors for every path\n• phasor rotates at f = E/h; probability ∝ (resultant amplitude)²\n• single photons/electrons sent one at a time still build up an interference pattern\n• so the same model applies to both; the article's claim is too simple, the reply is supported\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` }
    ]
  },
  'B5': {
    name: 'Rise and Fall of the Clockwork Universe',
    questions: [
      { q: `A 470 μF capacitor is charged to 12 V and then discharged through a 100 kΩ resistor.\n\nCalculate the time constant of the circuit.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `τ = RC = 100 × 10³ × 470 × 10⁻⁶ = 47 s  B1\nNot 47 without unit; Allow 47.0 s` },
      { q: `A capacitor is charged from a 6.0 V supply through a 10 kΩ resistor. A student displays the current I against time t on a data logger. The graph is a decaying curve starting at 0.60 mA.\n\nState what physical quantity is represented by the area between the curve and the time axis.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `The charge (stored on / that has flowed onto) the capacitor  B1\nAllow Q; Not energy` },
      { q: `A capacitor of capacitance 1000 μF holds an initial charge of 5.0 mC. It discharges through a resistor so that the time constant RC is 10 s. A student models the discharge in a spreadsheet using the iterative rule\nΔQ = −(Q / RC) Δt\nwith a time step Δt = 2.0 s.\n\nCalculate the charge predicted by the model after two time steps, and compare it with the value given by Q = Q₀e^(−t/RC) at t = 4.0 s.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `First step: ΔQ = −(5.0 / 10) × 2.0 = −1.0 mC so Q = 4.0 mC  C1\nSecond step: ΔQ = −(4.0 / 10) × 2.0 = −0.80 mC  C1\nQ = 3.2 mC after 4.0 s  A1\nExact: Q = 5.0 e^(−0.40) = 3.35 mC, so the model gives a value that is too low (by about 0.15 mC / 5%)  B1\nAllow 3.4 mC for exact value; ECF from incorrect step values` },
      { q: `A spreadsheet model of capacitor discharge uses the rule ΔQ = −(Q / RC) Δt, working out the current from the charge at the start of each time step. With a time step of 0.2RC the model always predicts a smaller charge than the exponential equation.\n\nExplain why the model underestimates the charge and how the model could be made more accurate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The current (rate of loss of charge) is calculated at the start of each step, where it is largest; in reality it decreases during the step, so too much charge is removed each step  B1\nUse a smaller time step (Δt much less than RC) so the current changes less during each step  B1` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA student discharges a capacitor through a 22 kΩ resistor and records the potential difference V across it with a data logger.\n\nt / s:  0,  10,  20,  30,  40,  50\nV / V:  9.00,  5.71,  3.63,  2.30,  1.46,  0.93\n\nDetermine the capacitance of the capacitor using a graphical method based on ln V.`, marks: 3, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `ln V values: 2.197, 1.742, 1.289, 0.833, 0.378, −0.073 (straight line against t)  C1\nGradient = −(2.197 − (−0.073)) / 50 = −0.0454 s⁻¹ = −1/RC so RC = 22.0 s  C1\nC = 22.0 / 22 000 = 1.0 × 10⁻³ F (1000 μF)  A1\nAllow gradient from any two well-separated values; Allow 0.98–1.02 × 10⁻³ F` },
      { q: `In a capacitor discharge experiment a student connects a voltmeter directly across the capacitor. The discharge resistor is 470 kΩ. The student's measured time constant is noticeably smaller than the value of RC calculated from the component labels.\n\nSuggest why, and state what property the voltmeter should have.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `The voltmeter is in parallel with the resistor and provides another path for charge to flow, so the effective resistance (and the time constant) is smaller  B1\nThe voltmeter should have a resistance very much larger than 470 kΩ (e.g. a high-resistance digital meter / data logger)  B1` },
      { q: `The flash unit of a camera uses a 220 μF capacitor charged to 330 V. The capacitor is fully discharged through the flash tube in each flash.\n\nCalculate the energy transferred in one flash.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `E = ½CV² = ½ × 220 × 10⁻⁶ × 330²  C1\nE = 12 J  A1\nAllow 11.98 J; Note: CV² = 24 J scores 1 mark` },
      { q: `A domestic smoke detector contains a source of americium-241 with an activity of 37 kBq. The half-life of americium-241 is 432 years. Take 1 year = 3.16 × 10⁷ s.\n\nCalculate the number of americium-241 nuclei in the source.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `λ = ln 2 / t½ = 0.693 / (432 × 3.16 × 10⁷)  C1\nλ = 5.08 × 10⁻¹¹ s⁻¹  C1\nN = A / λ = 3.7 × 10⁴ / 5.08 × 10⁻¹¹ = 7.3 × 10¹⁴  A1\nNote: failing to convert years to seconds gives 2.3 × 10⁷, scores 1 mark` },
      { q: `A student is asked to plan an experiment to test the claim that the discharge of a capacitor through a resistor is exponential, and to find the time constant of the circuit. Available apparatus: a capacitor of about 2200 μF, resistors, a 6 V supply, a switch, a voltmeter or data logger, and a stopwatch.\n\nDescribe how the student should carry out the experiment and analyse the results to test the claim and determine the time constant, including how to make the results reliable.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nMethod\n• circuit: supply charges capacitor, then switch to discharge through resistor; voltmeter/data logger across capacitor\n• choose R so that RC is tens of seconds (e.g. 22 kΩ gives about 50 s) so readings can be taken by hand, or use a data logger at a fixed sampling rate\n• record V at equal time intervals (e.g. every 5 s) over several time constants\nAnalysis\n• exponential test: ratio of successive V values at equal time intervals is constant / equal times to halve\n• plot ln V against t: a straight line confirms exponential decay\n• gradient = −1/RC, so time constant = −1/gradient; or time to fall to 0.37 V₀\nReliability and errors\n• repeat the discharge and average; start timing exactly at switch-over\n• voltmeter resistance much larger than R; account for tolerance of components (e.g. ±10% on C)\n• compare measured RC with value from component labels, with percentage difference\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A mass of 0.40 kg hangs from a spring of stiffness 25 N m⁻¹. It is pulled down 0.030 m from equilibrium and released, and oscillates with simple harmonic motion.\n\nCalculate the period of the oscillation and the maximum speed of the mass.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `T = 2π√(m/k) = 2π√(0.40/25) = 0.79 s  B1\nω = 2π/T = √(k/m) = 7.9 rad s⁻¹  C1\nv(max) = ωA = 7.9 × 0.030 = 0.24 m s⁻¹  A1\nECF from incorrect T` },
      { q: `A trolley of mass 0.25 kg is held between two springs on a horizontal air track. The combined stiffness of the springs is 40 N m⁻¹. The trolley is displaced 0.060 m and released. Friction is negligible.\n\nCalculate the total energy of the oscillation and the speed of the trolley when its displacement is 0.030 m.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Total energy = ½kA² = ½ × 40 × 0.060² = 0.072 J  A1\nPotential energy at x = 0.030 m: ½ × 40 × 0.030² = 0.018 J  C1\nKinetic energy = 0.072 − 0.018 = 0.054 J  C1\nv = √(2 × 0.054 / 0.25) = 0.66 m s⁻¹  A1\nAllow v = ω√(A² − x²) with ω = 12.6 rad s⁻¹ for last 3 marks` },
      { q: `A student pulls a mass on a spring down by 2 cm and then by 4 cm and finds that the period of oscillation is the same in each case.\n\nExplain, in terms of the restoring force, why the period does not depend on the amplitude.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Restoring force (and acceleration) is proportional to displacement: F = −kx, a = −(k/m)x  B1\nAt larger amplitude the mass has further to travel but its acceleration and speeds are proportionally larger, so the time for one cycle is unchanged  B1` },
      { q: `A computational model of a mass–spring oscillator uses k/m = 100 s⁻² and a time step Δt = 0.010 s. At t = 0, x = 0.050 m and v = 0. Each step uses:\na = −(k/m)x,  new v = v + aΔt,  new x = x + (new v)Δt\n\nCalculate the displacement predicted by the model after two time steps.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Step 1: a = −100 × 0.050 = −5.0 m s⁻²; v = −0.050 m s⁻¹  C1\nx = 0.050 − 0.050 × 0.010 = 0.0495 m  C1\nStep 2: a = −4.95 m s⁻²; v = −0.050 − 0.0495 = −0.0995 m s⁻¹  C1\nx = 0.0495 − 0.000995 = 0.0485 m  A1\nAllow 0.04851 m; ECF within steps` },
      { q: `A small boy on a playground swing is pushed by his father once every cycle. The swing reaches a large amplitude.\n\nState the condition for the driving force to produce the largest amplitude.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `The driving frequency equals the natural frequency of the swing (resonance)  B1\nAllow pushes timed to the natural period / in phase with the velocity` },
      { q: `When a car's engine runs at one particular speed a rear-view mirror vibrates with a large amplitude, making the image blurred. At engine speeds above and below this the vibration is small. The manufacturer fits a rubber mounting to the mirror.\n\nExplain why the mirror vibrates strongly at one engine speed and how the rubber mounting reduces the problem.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The engine provides a periodic driving force whose frequency depends on engine speed  B1\nAt one speed the driving frequency equals the natural frequency of the mirror, so resonance occurs and energy is transferred efficiently, giving a large amplitude  B1\nRubber damps the oscillation / dissipates energy each cycle, so the amplitude at resonance is reduced (and the resonance peak is broader)  B1` },
      { q: `A student measures the period of a simple pendulum with a hand-held stopwatch by timing 20 complete oscillations rather than one.\n\nState one reason why this improves the measurement.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Reduces the percentage uncertainty in the period (the reaction-time uncertainty is spread over 20 oscillations)  B1\nNot "more accurate" alone` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA student measures the length of a simple pendulum as 0.820 ± 0.002 m. The time for 20 oscillations is 36.4 ± 0.2 s. The period is given by T = 2π√(L/g).\n\nCalculate a value for g and its absolute uncertainty.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `T = 36.4/20 = 1.82 s; g = 4π²L/T² = 4π² × 0.820 / 1.82²  C1\ng = 9.77 m s⁻²  A1\n% uncertainty = 0.24% + 2 × 0.55% = 1.3%  C1\nabsolute uncertainty = ± 0.13 m s⁻² (g = 9.8 ± 0.1 m s⁻²)  A1\nNote: not doubling the uncertainty in T gives ± 0.08 m s⁻², scores 3 marks` },
      { q: `A car of mass 1200 kg travels at a constant speed of 15 m s⁻¹ around a flat bend that is an arc of a circle of radius 50 m.\n\nCalculate the resultant force on the car and state its direction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `F = mv²/r = 1200 × 15² / 50 = 5400 N  B1\nTowards the centre of the circle (provided by friction between tyres and road)  B1` },
      { q: `A 1000 kg satellite is launched from the Earth's surface into an orbit 400 km above the surface. Mass of Earth = 5.97 × 10²⁴ kg, radius of Earth = 6.37 × 10⁶ m, G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻².\n\nCalculate the gain in gravitational potential energy of the satellite.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `V = −GM/r: at surface −6.25 × 10⁷ J kg⁻¹; at orbit (r = 6.77 × 10⁶ m) −5.88 × 10⁷ J kg⁻¹  C1\nΔV = 3.7 × 10⁶ J kg⁻¹  C1\nΔE = mΔV = 3.7 × 10⁹ J  A1\nNote: using mgh with g = 9.81 gives 3.9 × 10⁹ J, scores 1 mark` },
      { q: `A space probe is to leave the Earth without further propulsion. Mass of Earth = 5.97 × 10²⁴ kg, radius of Earth = 6.37 × 10⁶ m, G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻².\n\nShow that the escape velocity from the Earth's surface is about 11 km s⁻¹.`, marks: 2, command: 'Show', markScheme: `½mv² = GMm/r so v = √(2GM/r) = √(2 × 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 5.97 × 10²⁴ / 6.37 × 10⁶)  C1\nv = 1.12 × 10⁴ m s⁻¹ (11.2 km s⁻¹)  A1\nAnswer must be given to at least 3 s.f.` },
      { q: `Io, one of Jupiter's moons, moves in a circular orbit of radius 4.22 × 10⁸ m with a period of 1.77 days. G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻².\n\nDetermine the mass of Jupiter.`, marks: 3, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `T = 1.77 × 86 400 = 1.53 × 10⁵ s  C1\nM = 4π²r³ / GT² = 4π² × (4.22 × 10⁸)³ / (6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × (1.53 × 10⁵)²)  C1\nM = 1.9 × 10²⁷ kg  A1` },
      { q: `GPS satellites move in circular orbits around the Earth with a period of 11.97 hours. Mass of Earth = 5.97 × 10²⁴ kg, radius of Earth = 6.37 × 10⁶ m, G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻².\n\nShow that T² = (4π²/GM)r³ for a circular orbit and hence calculate the height of a GPS satellite above the Earth's surface.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Gravitational force provides centripetal force: GMm/r² = mv²/r  M1\nv = 2πr/T substituted, giving T² = 4π²r³/GM  A1\nT = 11.97 × 3600 = 4.31 × 10⁴ s  C1\nr = (GMT²/4π²)^(1/3) = 2.66 × 10⁷ m  C1\nheight = 2.66 × 10⁷ − 6.37 × 10⁶ = 2.0 × 10⁷ m  A1\nNote: answer 2.66 × 10⁷ m (radius not height) scores 4 marks` },
      { q: `A satellite moves at constant speed in a circular orbit around the Earth. The gravitational force on it is always towards the Earth's centre.\n\nState why the gravitational force does no work on the satellite.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `The force is perpendicular to the velocity (direction of motion), so there is no displacement in the direction of the force  B1\nAllow: satellite stays on an equipotential, so no change in potential energy` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nThe hydrogen-alpha line has a wavelength of 656.3 nm in the laboratory. In the spectrum of a distant galaxy the same line is observed at 669.4 nm. Take the Hubble constant H₀ = 70 km s⁻¹ Mpc⁻¹.\n\nCalculate the distance to the galaxy in Mpc.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `z = Δλ/λ = 13.1 / 656.3 = 0.0200  C1\nv = zc = 0.0200 × 3.00 × 10⁸ = 6.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ (6000 km s⁻¹)  C1\nd = v/H₀ = 6.0 × 10³ / 70 = 86 Mpc  A1\nAllow 85–86 Mpc` },
      { q: `Measurements of galaxies give a Hubble constant of 70 km s⁻¹ Mpc⁻¹. 1 Mpc = 3.09 × 10²² m and 1 year = 3.16 × 10⁷ s.\n\nEstimate the age of the universe in years.`, marks: 2, command: 'Estimate', markScheme: `H₀ = 70 × 10³ / 3.09 × 10²² = 2.27 × 10⁻¹⁸ s⁻¹  C1\nage ≈ 1/H₀ = 4.4 × 10¹⁷ s = 1.4 × 10¹⁰ years  A1` },
      { q: `Muons are created in the upper atmosphere and travel towards the ground at 0.995c. A muon at rest has a mean lifetime of 2.2 μs. c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.\n\nCalculate the mean distance travelled by these muons in the Earth's frame of reference.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `γ = 1/√(1 − v²/c²) = 1/√(1 − 0.995²) = 10.0  C1\nlifetime in Earth frame = 10.0 × 2.2 μs = 22 μs  C1\ndistance = 0.995 × 3.00 × 10⁸ × 22 × 10⁻⁶ = 6.6 × 10³ m  A1\nNote: ignoring time dilation gives 660 m, scores 0` },
      { q: `Proxima Centauri, the nearest star to the Sun, is 4.24 light-years away. c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and 1 year = 3.16 × 10⁷ s.\n\nCalculate this distance in metres.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `4.24 × 3.00 × 10⁸ × 3.16 × 10⁷ = 4.0 × 10¹⁶ m  B1` },
      { q: `Atomic clocks on board GPS satellites move at about 3.9 km s⁻¹ relative to receivers on the ground. Engineers apply a correction for special relativity to the satellite clocks.\n\nExplain why the satellite clocks need this correction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Clocks moving relative to an observer run slow (time dilation, t = γτ)  B1\nSo, as seen from the ground, the satellite clocks lose time; even a tiny drift gives large position errors because the signals travel at c  B1\nAllow: γ is very slightly greater than 1` },
      { q: `Astronomers claim that the universe is expanding and that it has a finite age.\n\nExplain how measurements of the light from distant galaxies support this claim and how they are used to estimate the age of the universe. Include the limitations of the estimate.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nObservations\n• spectral lines of known elements from galaxies are shifted to longer wavelengths (redshift)\n• z = Δλ/λ ≈ v/c gives recession speed\n• distances found independently (e.g. standard candles such as Cepheids or supernovae)\nInterpretation\n• recession speed is proportional to distance: v = H₀d (Hubble's law)\n• all distant galaxies recede, consistent with space expanding\n• running the expansion backwards suggests everything was together at one time\nAge and limitations\n• time = d/v = 1/H₀, about 14 billion years for H₀ ≈ 70 km s⁻¹ Mpc⁻¹\n• assumes constant expansion rate; rate may have changed (deceleration/acceleration)\n• uncertainties in distance measurements give uncertainty in H₀; local motions of nearby galaxies add scatter\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A bicycle tyre contains 0.012 m³ of air at a pressure of 2.4 × 10⁵ Pa and a temperature of 290 K. Treat the air as an ideal gas. R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹, Nₐ = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹.\n\nCalculate the number of molecules of air in the tyre.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `n = pV/RT = 2.4 × 10⁵ × 0.012 / (8.31 × 290) = 1.2 mol  C1\nN = 1.2 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 7.2 × 10²³  A1\nAllow N = pV/kT directly` },
      { q: `The tyre of a car is driven on a hot day. The volume of the air in the tyre stays almost constant but its temperature rises.\n\nExplain, using the kinetic model of a gas, why the pressure in the tyre increases.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Higher temperature means greater mean kinetic energy / mean square speed of the molecules  B1\nMolecules hit the walls more often and each collision gives a greater change of momentum  B1\nSo greater rate of change of momentum, greater force per unit area on the walls (pressure = force/area)  B1` },
      { q: `Air is mostly nitrogen. The molar mass of nitrogen (N₂) is 0.028 kg mol⁻¹. k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹, Nₐ = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹.\n\nCalculate the root mean square speed of nitrogen molecules at 300 K.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `m = 0.028 / 6.02 × 10²³ = 4.65 × 10⁻²⁶ kg  C1\n½m⟨c²⟩ = (3/2)kT so ⟨c²⟩ = 3 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 300 / 4.65 × 10⁻²⁶  C1\nc(rms) = 520 m s⁻¹  A1\nAllow 517 m s⁻¹` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA student traps a fixed mass of air in a syringe connected to a pressure sensor. The temperature stays at 293 K. Results:\n\nV / cm³:  40,  35,  30,  25,  20\np / kPa:  100,  114,  133,  160,  200\n\nDetermine whether the data are consistent with Boyle's law, and calculate the amount of air in moles. R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `pV calculated for each row: 4000, 3990, 3990, 4000, 4000 kPa cm³  C1\npV is constant (within 0.25%), so p ∝ 1/V, consistent with Boyle's law  B1\npV = 4000 × 10³ Pa × 10⁻⁶ m³ = 4.0 J  C1\nn = pV/RT = 4.0 / (8.31 × 293) = 1.6 × 10⁻³ mol  A1\nNote: unit error in pV conversion loses the last two marks` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA flask of air of fixed volume is connected to a pressure gauge and heated in a water bath.\n\nθ / °C:  0,  20,  40,  60,  80,  100\np / kPa:  101.0,  108.6,  116.2,  123.8,  131.4,  139.0\n\nDetermine a value for absolute zero in °C from these data, calculate its percentage difference from −273 °C, and suggest one reason for the difference.`, marks: 5, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `Gradient = (139.0 − 101.0) / 100 = 0.380 kPa K⁻¹  C1\nPressure would be zero when θ = −101.0 / 0.380  C1\nAbsolute zero ≈ −266 °C  A1\n% difference = 7/273 × 100 ≈ 2.6%  B1\nPart of the air (e.g. in the connecting tube) is not at the water-bath temperature / air not in thermal equilibrium with the water when read / flask expands  B1\nAllow −265 to −267 °C` },
      { q: `An electron in a semiconductor needs 0.50 eV to be freed from its atom. The semiconductor is at 300 K. k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹, e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.\n\nCalculate the Boltzmann factor for this process.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `E/kT = 0.50 × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ / (1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 300) = 19.3  C1\ne^(−19.3) = 4.1 × 10⁻⁹  A1` },
      { q: `A chemical reaction has an activation energy of 0.55 eV per molecule. A common rule says that the rate of such reactions near room temperature roughly doubles for a 10 K rise. k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹, e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.\n\nShow, using the Boltzmann factor, that the rate approximately doubles when the temperature rises from 300 K to 310 K.`, marks: 4, command: 'Show', markScheme: `E = 0.55 × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 8.8 × 10⁻²⁰ J  C1\nAt 300 K: e^(−E/kT) = e^(−21.3) = 5.9 × 10⁻¹⁰  C1\nAt 310 K: e^(−20.6) = 1.17 × 10⁻⁹  C1\nRatio = 1.17 × 10⁻⁹ / 5.9 × 10⁻¹⁰ = 1.99 ≈ 2, and rate ∝ Boltzmann factor  A1\nAllow ratio = exp[(E/k)(1/300 − 1/310)] = e^(0.686)` },
      { q: `Sweat on a runner's skin evaporates even though the skin is well below 100 °C.\n\nExplain why evaporation of sweat cools the skin.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Molecules have a range of energies; only those with energy greater than the energy needed to escape (the fastest) leave the surface  B1\nSo the mean kinetic energy of the remaining liquid falls, lowering its temperature, and energy flows from the skin to the liquid  B1` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA student uses a 12 V immersion heater, carrying a current of 4.0 A, to heat a 1.00 kg aluminium block for 600 s. The temperature of the block rises by 31.5 K.\n\nCalculate the specific heat capacity of aluminium from these results.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Energy = VIt = 12 × 4.0 × 600 = 2.88 × 10⁴ J  C1\nc = E / mΔθ = 2.88 × 10⁴ / (1.00 × 31.5)  C1\nc = 910 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹  A1\nAllow 914 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹` },
      { q: `In an experiment to find the specific heat capacity of a metal block with an electrical heater, a student's value is higher than the accepted value.\n\nSuggest a reason for this and one improvement to the method.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Energy is lost to the surroundings, so the temperature rise is smaller than it would be, giving a larger c  B1\nInsulate the block (lagging / polystyrene) or use a smaller temperature rise centred on room temperature / add oil in the thermometer hole for good thermal contact  B1` },
      { q: `Typical thermal energies of particles are of order kT. A laboratory is at 293 K. k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹, e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.\n\nCalculate kT at this temperature in electronvolts.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `kT = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 293 / 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 0.025 eV  B1\nAllow 1/40 eV` },
      { q: `The specific latent heat of vaporisation of water is 2.26 × 10⁶ J kg⁻¹ and its molar mass is 0.018 kg mol⁻¹. k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹, Nₐ = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹.\n\nEstimate the energy needed for one molecule to leave liquid water, and use it to estimate how many times larger the Boltzmann factor for evaporation is at 373 K than at 293 K. Comment on your answer.`, marks: 5, command: 'Estimate', markScheme: `E per molecule = 2.26 × 10⁶ × 0.018 / 6.02 × 10²³  C1\nE = 6.8 × 10⁻²⁰ J  A1\nAt 373 K: e^(−13.1) = 2.0 × 10⁻⁶; at 293 K: e^(−16.7) = 5.5 × 10⁻⁸  C1\nRatio ≈ 36 (allow 30–40)  A1\nComment: a fraction of molecules has enough energy at room temperature so water evaporates slowly; the rate rises steeply with temperature  B1` }
    ]
  },
  'B6': {
    name: 'Field and Particle Physics',
    questions: [
      { q: `A student pushes a bar magnet into a coil connected to a sensitive ammeter. The current induced in the coil sets up a magnetic field that opposes the motion of the magnet, so the student has to do work to push the magnet in.\n\nState which conservation law requires the induced current to oppose the change that produces it.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Conservation of energy  B1\n(The electrical energy dissipated in the circuit comes from the work done pushing the magnet.)\nNot: conservation of charge / momentum` },
      { q: `A search coil is connected to an oscilloscope. It is placed at the centre of a long solenoid that carries a steady direct current of 2.0 A.\n\nState the reading on the oscilloscope while the current stays steady.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Zero (no induced emf) because the flux linkage of the search coil is not changing  B1\nNote: the reason is needed for the mark.` },
      { q: `A data logger records the flux linkage NΦ of a coil as a magnet is moved near it, and plots a graph of NΦ (in Wb turns) against time t (in s).\n\nState the quantity given by the gradient of this graph at any instant.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `(Magnitude of) the induced emf (in V)  B1\nAllow: −emf / ε = −d(NΦ)/dt\nNot: flux / current` },
      { q: `A small a.c. generator has a flat coil rotating at constant frequency in a uniform magnetic field. At one instant the plane of the coil is perpendicular to the field lines, so the flux linkage of the coil is at its maximum value.\n\nExplain why the emf induced in the coil is zero at this instant.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `emf = −rate of change of flux linkage (Faraday's law)  B1\nAt the maximum of NΦ (a turning point of the cosine curve) the rate of change of flux linkage is zero, so emf = 0  B1\nAllow: the sides of the coil are moving parallel to the field lines, so they cut no flux` },
      { q: `The core of a mains transformer is made from thin sheets of iron (laminations) that are insulated from one another, rather than from a solid block of iron.\n\nExplain how laminating the core improves the efficiency of the transformer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The changing flux in the core induces emfs and hence eddy currents in the iron  B1\nInsulating layers break up the current paths / increase resistance so eddy currents are much smaller, so less energy is dissipated as heat in the core  B1` },
      { q: `A small d.c. motor is connected to a 12.0 V supply. The resistance of the armature coil is 0.80 Ω. When the motor is running at full speed the current in it is 2.0 A.\n\nCalculate the back emf induced in the armature coil at full speed.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `12.0 = ε_back + 2.0 × 0.80  C1\nε_back = 10.4 V  A1\nAllow: 10 V\nNote: 1.6 V scores 1 mark (p.d. across the coil resistance only)` },
      { q: `A student places a search coil connected to an oscilloscope next to a coil carrying an alternating current from a signal generator. The amplitude of the current is kept constant. At a frequency of 200 Hz the peak emf of the search coil is 24 mV.\n\nPredict the peak emf when the frequency is increased to 400 Hz, and explain your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `48 mV  B1\nThe peak flux linkage is unchanged but it changes twice as fast, so the (maximum) rate of change of flux linkage, and hence the emf, doubles  B1` },
      { q: `A rectangular coil of 120 turns measures 4.0 cm × 2.5 cm. It is held with its plane perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field of flux density 0.080 T. The coil is then pulled completely out of the field in a time of 0.15 s.\n\nCalculate the average emf induced in the coil.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `A = 0.040 × 0.025 = 1.0 × 10⁻³ m²  C1\nNΦ = 120 × 0.080 × 1.0 × 10⁻³ = 9.6 × 10⁻³ Wb turns  C1\nε = 9.6 × 10⁻³ / 0.15 = 0.064 V  A1\nAllow: 64 mV\nNote: 5.3 × 10⁻⁴ V scores 2 marks (N omitted)` },
      { q: `A coil in a small a.c. generator rotates at a steady frequency of 50 Hz in a uniform magnetic field. The maximum flux linkage of the coil is 0.090 Wb turns.\n\nSketch, on the same time axis covering two complete cycles, a graph of the flux linkage of the coil and a graph of the induced emf against time, starting when the flux linkage is a maximum. Label the time of one complete cycle.`, marks: 3, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `Flux linkage drawn as a cosine curve starting at a maximum of 0.090 Wb turns, two cycles  B1\nemf drawn as a sine-shaped curve 90° (a quarter cycle) out of phase, zero when NΦ is a maximum and maximum when NΦ is zero, sign consistent with ε = −d(NΦ)/dt (emf goes negative first)  B1\nPeriod marked as 0.020 s (20 ms)  B1\nAllow: emf curve starting positive if no sign convention is assumed, for the second mark only if the phase is correct` },
      { q: `A strong neodymium magnet is dropped down a vertical copper tube. It takes much longer to reach the bottom than an identical unmagnetised piece of metal dropped down the same tube. Copper is not attracted to magnets.\n\nExplain why the magnet falls slowly.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `As the magnet falls, the flux linking each section (ring) of the tube changes  B1\nThis induces emfs and so eddy currents in the copper (copper is a good conductor)  B1\nBy Lenz's law the eddy currents produce magnetic fields that oppose the motion of the magnet, giving an upward force (so it soon reaches a low terminal speed)  B1\nAllow: kinetic energy is transferred to thermal energy in the tube` },
      { q: `A phone charger uses a transformer to step the 230 V r.m.s. mains supply down to 12 V r.m.s. The primary coil has 1150 turns. The transformer may be assumed to be ideal.\n\nCalculate the number of turns on the secondary coil and the current in the primary coil when the secondary current is 2.0 A.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `N_s = 1150 × 12 / 230  C1\nN_s = 60  A1\nI_p = 12 × 2.0 / 230 = 0.10 A  B1\nAllow: 0.104 A; ECF for I_p from an incorrect turns ratio` },
      { q: `An iron ring forms a magnetic circuit of length 0.32 m and cross-sectional area 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ m². The permeability of the iron is 1.26 × 10⁻³ H m⁻¹. A coil of 400 turns carrying a current of 0.50 A is wound on the ring.\n\nCalculate the permeance of the ring and the magnetic flux density in the iron.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Permeance Λ = μA/L = 1.26 × 10⁻³ × 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ / 0.32  C1\nΛ = 1.6 × 10⁻⁶ Wb A⁻¹ (H)  A1\nΦ = Λ × NI = 1.6 × 10⁻⁶ × 400 × 0.50 = 3.1 × 10⁻⁴ Wb  C1\nB = Φ/A = 0.79 T  A1\nAllow: 1.58 × 10⁻⁶ or 1.57 × 10⁻⁶ H; 0.78–0.79 T\nECF from Λ` },
      { q: `An iron ring has a permeance of 1.57 × 10⁻⁶ H. A cut is made in the ring to leave an air gap of length 1.0 mm. The cross-sectional area of the gap is 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ m². The permeability of free space μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ H m⁻¹. The magnetising coil has 200 current-turns in both cases.\n\nShow that the air gap reduces the flux in the ring by a factor of about 4.`, marks: 3, command: 'Show', markScheme: `Λ_gap = μ₀A/L = 4π × 10⁻⁷ × 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ / 1.0 × 10⁻³ = 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ H  C1\nPermeances in series: 1/Λ = 1/1.57 × 10⁻⁶ + 1/5.03 × 10⁻⁷ → Λ = 3.8 × 10⁻⁷ H  C1\nRatio = 1.57 × 10⁻⁶ / 3.81 × 10⁻⁷ = 4.1 (flux ∝ permeance for the same current-turns)  A1\nAllow: comparing fluxes 3.1 × 10⁻⁴ Wb and 7.6 × 10⁻⁵ Wb\nNote: adding permeances directly scores 0 for the last two marks` },
      { q: `A student measures the magnetic flux density between the poles of a magnet. The magnet sits on a top-pan balance and a horizontal wire is clamped so that 5.0 cm of it lies between the poles, at right angles to the field. The balance is zeroed with no current, and the reading is recorded for different currents.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\nI / A: 0.0, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0\nbalance reading / g: 0.00, 0.26, 0.51, 0.77, 1.02\n\nUse the data to determine the flux density of the field. g = 9.81 N kg⁻¹.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `Gradient of reading against I (or use of the 4.0 A point on the straight line) = 0.255 g A⁻¹  C1\nForce per amp = 0.255 × 10⁻³ × 9.81 = 2.50 × 10⁻³ N A⁻¹  C1\nF = BIL so B = (F/I)/L = 2.50 × 10⁻³ / 0.050  C1\nB = 0.050 T  A1\nAllow: 0.049–0.052 T\nNote: answer of 5.0 T scores 3 marks (L used in cm); use of a single non-zero point other than the gradient allowed if correct` },
      { q: `In an experiment to measure the force on a current-carrying wire, the top-pan balance has a resolution of 0.01 g. The balance reading is 0.26 g at a current of 1.0 A and 1.02 g at a current of 4.0 A.\n\nCalculate the percentage uncertainty in the reading at 1.0 A and explain why the student should use larger currents.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `0.01/0.26 × 100 = 3.8%  B1\nAt 4.0 A the percentage uncertainty is only about 1% (0.01/1.02), so larger readings give smaller percentage uncertainty in F and hence in B  B1\nAllow: 4%` },
      { q: `A student compares the electric field around a charged sphere with the gravitational field around the Earth. Both fields are radial and both field strengths obey an inverse square law with distance.\n\nState one difference between electric and gravitational fields.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Any one:\nElectric forces can be attractive or repulsive; gravitational forces are only attractive  B1\nOR electric field acts on charge, gravitational field acts on mass\nOR electric field can be shielded / is much stronger for particles such as protons` },
      { q: `Two horizontal parallel metal plates are 4.0 cm apart. A potential difference of 2000 V is applied between them.\n\nCalculate the electric field strength between the plates and the force on an electron in this field.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `E = V/d = 2000 / 0.040 = 5.0 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹  B1\nF = eE = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 5.0 × 10⁴ = 8.0 × 10⁻¹⁵ N  B1\nAllow: N C⁻¹ for V m⁻¹; ECF for F` },
      { q: `The dome of a Van de Graaff generator is a conducting sphere of radius 0.15 m. When it is charged its potential is 150 kV. The charge on the dome can be treated as a point charge at its centre. k = 1/4πε₀ = 8.99 × 10⁹ N m² C⁻².\n\nCalculate the electric field strength at the surface of the dome.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Q = Vr/k = 150 × 10³ × 0.15 / 8.99 × 10⁹ = 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ C, then E = kQ/r²  C1\nOR E = V/r for a sphere\nE = 1.0 × 10⁶ V m⁻¹  A1` },
      { q: `In a linear accelerator, protons pass through a line of hollow metal drift tubes connected alternately to the terminals of a high-frequency alternating supply. The protons are accelerated in the gaps between the tubes and the supply frequency is fixed.\n\nExplain why the drift tubes must get longer along the accelerator.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The protons gain speed (energy) at each gap  B1\nThe time spent inside each tube must be the same (half a period of the supply) so that the p.d. has reversed when the protons reach the next gap; faster protons travel further in that time  B1` },
      { q: `In a cathode ray tube, an electron beam enters a uniform magnetic field directed at right angles to its velocity. The beam follows a circular arc.\n\nExplain why the electrons move in a circle at constant speed.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The force F = qvB is always perpendicular to the velocity (so it provides a centripetal force)  B1\nNo component of force along the direction of motion, so no work is done and the kinetic energy (speed) is constant; constant-size force perpendicular to v gives a circle  B1` },
      { q: `An electron gun in an electron diffraction tube accelerates electrons from rest through a potential difference of 2.0 kV. The mass of an electron is 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg.\n\nShow that the speed of the electrons leaving the gun is about 2.7 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹.`, marks: 3, command: 'Show', markScheme: `Kinetic energy = eV = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 2000 = 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁶ J  C1\n½ × 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ × v² = 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁶  C1\nv = 2.65 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹  A1\nNote: final answer must be given to at least 3 s.f.; non-relativistic treatment is acceptable at this speed` },
      { q: `A student investigates Coulomb's law using two small charged conducting spheres, one on an insulating rod and one hanging from an insulating thread. The force between them is found from the deflection of the hanging sphere. The charge on each sphere is the same and does not change during the experiment.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\nseparation of centres r / cm: 4.0, 6.0, 8.0\nforce F / mN: 3.52, 1.56, 0.88\n\nShow that the data are consistent with an inverse square law and determine the charge on each sphere. k = 8.99 × 10⁹ N m² C⁻².`, marks: 3, command: 'Show', markScheme: `F r² calculated for all three: 56.3, 56.2, 56.3 (mN cm²), constant so F ∝ 1/r²  B1\nk q² = F r² = 56.3 × 10⁻³ × 10⁻⁴ = 5.63 × 10⁻⁶ N m²  C1\nq = √(5.63 × 10⁻⁶ / 8.99 × 10⁹) = 2.5 × 10⁻⁸ C  A1\nAllow: 25 nC; ratio method (e.g. doubling r from 4 to 8 cm quarters F) for the first mark` },
      { q: `In Rutherford's experiment, alpha particles of kinetic energy 5.0 MeV are fired head-on at a gold nucleus (proton number Z = 79). Treat the gold nucleus as a fixed point charge and ignore the electrons. k = 8.99 × 10⁹ N m² C⁻².\n\nCalculate the distance of closest approach of an alpha particle to the centre of the nucleus.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Kinetic energy = 5.0 × 10⁶ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 8.0 × 10⁻¹³ J  C1\nk(2e)(79e)/r = 8.0 × 10⁻¹³ → r = 8.99 × 10⁹ × 2 × 79 × (1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹)² / 8.0 × 10⁻¹³  C1\nr = 4.5 × 10⁻¹⁴ m  A1\nNote: 2.3 × 10⁻¹⁴ m scores 2 marks (charge of alpha taken as e)` },
      { q: `The Large Hadron Collider keeps protons on a circular path using bending magnets. The radius of the bending path is 2.80 km. Protons circulate with an energy of 6.5 TeV; at this energy the momentum of a proton is given by p = E/c.\n\nCalculate the magnetic flux density needed in the bending magnets.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `p = 6.5 × 10¹² × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ / 3.00 × 10⁸ = 3.5 × 10⁻¹⁵ kg m s⁻¹  C1\nr = p/(eB) so B = p/(er) = 3.47 × 10⁻¹⁵ / (1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 2800)  C1\nB = 7.7 T  A1\nNote: use of p = mv with rest mass gives an impossible speed and scores 0 for the first mark` },
      { q: `An electron moving horizontally at 2.65 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹ enters the region midway between two horizontal parallel plates. The plates are 5.0 cm long and the uniform field between them is 5.0 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹, directed vertically. The plates are 4.0 cm apart. Ignore gravity and edge effects. The mass of an electron is 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg.\n\nCalculate the vertical deflection of the electron as it leaves the plates, and describe the shape of its path between the plates.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `a = eE/m = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 5.0 × 10⁴ / 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ = 8.8 × 10¹⁵ m s⁻²  C1\nt = 0.050 / 2.65 × 10⁷ = 1.9 × 10⁻⁹ s (horizontal velocity constant)  C1\ny = ½at² = ½ × 8.78 × 10¹⁵ × (1.89 × 10⁻⁹)²  C1\ny = 0.016 m (1.6 cm) (less than 2.0 cm, so the electron clears the plate)  A1\nPath is a parabola: constant horizontal velocity with uniform vertical acceleration  B1\nECF from a and t` },
      { q: `A velocity selector in a mass spectrometer has a uniform electric field of 3.0 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹ at right angles to a uniform magnetic field of 0.15 T. Singly charged neon ions that pass undeflected then enter a region where only the 0.15 T magnetic field acts, and travel in a semicircle. The mass of a neon-20 ion is 20 u, where u = 1.661 × 10⁻²⁷ kg.\n\nCalculate the speed of the selected ions and the radius of the semicircle for neon-20, and state the radius for neon-22 ions.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Undeflected when eE = evB  C1\nv = E/B = 3.0 × 10⁴ / 0.15 = 2.0 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹  A1\nr = mv/(eB) = 20 × 1.661 × 10⁻²⁷ × 2.0 × 10⁵ / (1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 0.15)  C1\nr = 0.28 m  A1\nneon-22: r = 0.28 × 22/20 = 0.30 m (0.305 m)  B1\nECF from v` },
      { q: `A student lists four particles: proton, muon, pion, neutron.\n\nIdentify the particle in the list that is a lepton.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Muon  B1` },
      { q: `In a PET scanner, a positron emitted by a tracer travels a short distance and annihilates with an electron. Two gamma photons are produced. Just before annihilating, the positron and electron are almost at rest.\n\nState why the two photons travel in opposite directions.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Momentum is conserved: the initial momentum is (almost) zero so the photons must have equal and opposite momenta  B1` },
      { q: `Carbon-14 decays by beta-minus emission:\n¹⁴₆C → ¹⁴₇N + ⁰₋₁e + X\n\nIdentify particle X.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `(Electron) antineutrino  B1\nNot: neutrino` },
      { q: `In beta-minus decay of a nucleus, a neutron (udd) changes into a proton (uud).\n\nDeduce the change that happens at the quark level and show that charge is conserved.`, marks: 2, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `A down quark changes into an up quark: d → u + e⁻ + ν̄  B1\nCharge: −⅓ = +⅔ + (−1) + 0  B1` },
      { q: `Electrons in an accelerator reach a total energy of 50 GeV. The rest energy of an electron is 0.511 MeV.\n\nCalculate the relativistic factor γ for these electrons.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `γ = total energy / rest energy = 50 × 10⁹ / 0.511 × 10⁶  C1\nγ = 9.8 × 10⁴  A1\nNote: unit mismatch (e.g. 0.098) scores 1 mark` },
      { q: `The radius of a nucleus is given approximately by r = r₀A^⅓, where r₀ = 1.2 fm and A is the nucleon number. Gold-197 has A = 197. The mass of a nucleon is about 1.661 × 10⁻²⁷ kg.\n\nEstimate the radius and the density of a gold-197 nucleus.`, marks: 3, command: 'Estimate', markScheme: `r = 1.2 × 197^⅓ = 7.0 fm (6.98 × 10⁻¹⁵ m)  B1\nρ = 197 × 1.661 × 10⁻²⁷ / (4/3 π (6.98 × 10⁻¹⁵)³)  C1\nρ = 2.3 × 10¹⁷ kg m⁻³  A1\nAllow: 2 × 10¹⁷ kg m⁻³; ECF from r` },
      { q: `A gamma-ray photon passing close to a lead nucleus creates an electron–positron pair. The rest energy of an electron is 0.511 MeV.\n\nCalculate the minimum photon energy needed, and the maximum wavelength of the photon, and explain why the pair is created near a nucleus rather than in empty space.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `E_min = 2 × 0.511 = 1.02 MeV (1.64 × 10⁻¹³ J)  B1\nλ = hc/E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ / 1.64 × 10⁻¹³ = 1.2 × 10⁻¹² m  B1\nThe nucleus takes up some momentum so that both energy and momentum can be conserved (a photon alone cannot do this)  B1` },
      { q: `In a simple model, an electron is trapped in a one-dimensional box of length 0.30 nm. The electron forms a standing wave with a node at each end of the box. The mass of an electron is 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg.\n\nCalculate the kinetic energy, in eV, of the electron in its lowest energy state.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Lowest state: half a wavelength fits the box, λ = 2L = 0.60 nm  B1\np = h/λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ / 6.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ = 1.1 × 10⁻²⁴ kg m s⁻¹  C1\nE_k = p²/2m = (1.105 × 10⁻²⁴)² / (2 × 9.11 × 10⁻³¹) = 6.7 × 10⁻¹⁹ J  C1\nE_k = 4.2 eV  A1\nNote: λ = L gives 16.8 eV and scores 3 marks` },
      { q: `Electrons of energy 420 MeV are scattered by carbon-12 nuclei. The intensity of the scattered electrons shows a first minimum at a scattering angle of 51°. At this energy the de Broglie wavelength is λ = hc/E. For diffraction by a nucleus of diameter D, the first minimum occurs where sin θ = 1.22λ/D.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the de Broglie wavelength of the electrons and determine the diameter of a carbon-12 nucleus.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `E = 420 × 10⁶ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 6.72 × 10⁻¹¹ J and λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ / 6.72 × 10⁻¹¹  C1\nλ = 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁵ m  A1\nD = 1.22 × 2.96 × 10⁻¹⁵ / sin 51°  C1\nD = 4.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ m  A1\nECF from λ` },
      { q: `A student tests the absorption of radiation from a beta source using sheets of paper and aluminium of different thicknesses.\n\nState the material and approximate thickness that would stop almost all of the beta radiation.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Aluminium a few mm thick (about 3–5 mm)  B1` },
      { q: `Americium-241 (proton number 95) is used in smoke detectors. It decays by alpha emission to an isotope of neptunium (Np).\n\nWrite a balanced nuclear equation for this decay.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `²⁴¹₉₅Am → ²³⁷₉₃Np + ⁴₂He (or ⁴₂α)  B1 for alpha particle, B1 for 237 and 93 on Np` },
      { q: `A student uses a Geiger–Müller tube to measure the count rate from a small gamma source. After subtracting background, the corrected count rate is 360 counts per minute when the detector is 10 cm from the source.\n\nPredict the corrected count rate at 30 cm, and suggest one reason why the measured value might differ from your prediction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `Inverse square: 360 × (10/30)² = 40 counts per minute  B1\nAny one: the source is inside a capsule so the true distance is not the measured distance / the source is not a point source / random variation in counts (count for longer)  B1` },
      { q: `The Sun radiates energy at a rate of 3.83 × 10²⁶ W. This energy comes from nuclear fusion in its core.\n\nCalculate the rate at which the Sun's mass decreases as a result.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Δm/Δt = P/c² = 3.83 × 10²⁶ / (3.00 × 10⁸)²  C1\n= 4.3 × 10⁹ kg s⁻¹  A1` },
      { q: `One possible fission of uranium-235 is\n¹n + ²³⁵U → ¹⁴¹Ba + ⁹²Kr + 3 ¹n\nMasses: ²³⁵U 235.0439 u, ¹⁴¹Ba 140.9144 u, ⁹²Kr 91.9262 u, ¹n 1.0087 u. 1 u = 1.661 × 10⁻²⁷ kg.\n\nCalculate the energy released in this fission, in MeV.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Δm = (235.0439 + 1.0087) − (140.9144 + 91.9262 + 3 × 1.0087) = 0.1859 u  C1\nE = 0.1859 × 1.661 × 10⁻²⁷ × (3.00 × 10⁸)² = 2.78 × 10⁻¹¹ J  C1\nE = 174 MeV  A1\nAllow: 170–175 MeV; use of 931.5 MeV u⁻¹ (gives 173 MeV)\nNote: forgetting the extra two neutrons on the right gives a wrong Δm and scores 1 mark at most (for E = Δmc²)` },
      { q: `A worker's hand, of mass 0.40 kg, absorbs 6.0 × 10⁻⁵ J of energy from gamma radiation while handling a source. The radiation weighting factor is 1 for gamma radiation and 20 for alpha radiation.\n\nCalculate the absorbed dose and the equivalent dose to the hand, and the equivalent dose if the same energy had been absorbed from alpha radiation.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Absorbed dose = 6.0 × 10⁻⁵ / 0.40 = 1.5 × 10⁻⁴ Gy  B1\nEquivalent dose (gamma) = 1.5 × 10⁻⁴ Sv (0.15 mSv)  B1\nAlpha: 20 × 1.5 × 10⁻⁴ = 3.0 × 10⁻³ Sv (3.0 mSv)  B1\nNote: units Gy and Sv must be used correctly` },
      { q: `A helium-4 nucleus contains 2 protons and 2 neutrons.\nMasses: helium-4 nucleus 4.001506 u, proton 1.007276 u, neutron 1.008665 u. 1 u = 1.661 × 10⁻²⁷ kg.\n\nCalculate the binding energy per nucleon of helium-4, in MeV.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Δm = 2(1.007276) + 2(1.008665) − 4.001506 = 0.030376 u  C1\nΔm = 0.030376 × 1.661 × 10⁻²⁷ = 5.05 × 10⁻²⁹ kg, E = Δmc² = 4.54 × 10⁻¹² J  C1\nE = 28.4 MeV  C1\nPer nucleon = 7.1 MeV  A1\nAllow: use of 931.5 MeV u⁻¹ (gives 28.3 MeV); ECF throughout` },
      { q: `Radon gas from rocks gives the average person in the UK an equivalent dose of about 1.3 mSv per year.\n\n(data written for this practice question) Assume the risk of a fatal cancer is 5% per sievert and that the UK population is 67 million.\n\nEstimate the number of fatal cancers per year caused by radon in the UK, and suggest why this estimate is unreliable.`, marks: 4, command: 'Estimate', markScheme: `Risk per person per year = 1.3 × 10⁻³ × 0.05 = 6.5 × 10⁻⁵  C1\nNumber = 6.5 × 10⁻⁵ × 6.7 × 10⁷  C1\n≈ 4000–4400 per year  A1\nAny one: risk factor is extrapolated from high doses (linear, no threshold model may not hold at low dose) / radon doses are very uneven (some homes much higher) / risk depends on other factors such as smoking  B1` },
      { q: `A student measures the absorption of gamma radiation by lead. The background count rate is 24 counts per minute.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\nthickness of lead x / mm: 0, 5, 10, 15, 20\nmeasured count rate / counts per minute: 1224, 849, 591, 414, 292\n\nThe corrected count rate C is expected to follow C = C₀e^(−μx). Determine μ and the thickness of lead that halves the corrected count rate.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `Background subtracted: 1200, 825, 567, 390, 268  B1\nGradient of ln C against x (or ln(1200/268)/20)  C1\nμ = 0.075 mm⁻¹  A1\nx½ = ln 2 / μ = 9.2 mm  A1\nAllow: μ 0.073–0.077 mm⁻¹, x½ 9.0–9.5 mm; ECF from μ\nNote: no background correction gives μ ≈ 0.072 mm⁻¹ and scores max 3` },
      { q: `In a model of fusion in a star, two deuterium nuclei (each of charge +e) must come within about 3 × 10⁻¹⁵ m of each other to fuse. Assume the mean kinetic energy of a nucleus is (3/2)k_BT, where k_B = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹, and that each nucleus must have kinetic energy equal to the electrical potential energy at closest approach. k = 1/4πε₀ = 8.99 × 10⁹ N m² C⁻².\n\nEstimate the temperature needed for fusion, and explain why fusion actually occurs in the Sun at about 1.5 × 10⁷ K.`, marks: 5, command: 'Estimate', markScheme: `E_p = 8.99 × 10⁹ × (1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹)² / 3 × 10⁻¹⁵  C1\nE_p = 7.7 × 10⁻¹⁴ J (about 0.5 MeV)  A1\n(3/2) × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × T = 7.7 × 10⁻¹⁴  C1\nT = 3.7 × 10⁹ K  A1\nA small fraction of nuclei have energies far above the mean (distribution of speeds) and/or quantum tunnelling through the barrier, so fusion happens at a much lower temperature  B1\nAllow: if each nucleus is given half of E_p, T = 1.9 × 10⁹ K` },
      { q: `In Rutherford's experiment, a thin gold foil was bombarded with alpha particles in a vacuum. Later experiments used high-energy electrons to measure the size of nuclei more accurately.\n\nDescribe the main observations of the alpha-scattering experiment, explain how they lead to the nuclear model of the atom, and explain how the size of the nucleus can be estimated from alpha scattering and from electron scattering.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nObservations\n• most alpha particles pass straight through or with small deflections\n• a small fraction are deflected through large angles\n• a very few (about 1 in 10⁴) are scattered back by more than 90°\nExplanations\n• the atom is mostly empty space\n• mass and positive charge concentrated in a tiny nucleus\n• large deflections need a large repulsive Coulomb force, so a close approach to a concentrated charge\n• the number scattered at each angle fits a model of an inverse square force from a point charge\nSize estimates\n• at closest approach (head-on) kinetic energy = electrical potential energy kQq/r\n• this gives an upper limit (about 10⁻¹⁴ m for gold) because the alpha does not touch the nucleus\n• electrons are leptons and are not affected by the strong force\n• high-energy electrons have de Broglie wavelength λ = hc/E of about 10⁻¹⁵ m, comparable with nuclear diameters\n• a diffraction minimum at angle θ gives D from sin θ ≈ 1.22λ/D; r = r₀A^⅓\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A model railway needs a 6 V a.c. supply from the 230 V mains. An engineer designs a transformer with two coils wound on a closed, laminated iron core and tests it. The output power is found to be a little less than the input power.\n\nExplain how the transformer produces the 6 V output, explain why the engineer chose a closed, laminated iron core, and describe the causes of the energy losses.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nOperation\n• alternating current in the primary gives an alternating flux in the core\n• the same changing flux links the secondary coil\n• emf induced = −rate of change of flux linkage (Faraday's law)\n• V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p, so about 1 turn on the secondary for every 38 on the primary\nCore design\n• iron has a high permeability, so the core has high permeance and a large flux for each current-turn\n• a closed core with no air gap keeps the permeance high, as an air gap has very low permeance\n• the core guides nearly all the flux through both coils, so there is good flux linkage\n• laminations insulated from each other reduce eddy currents\nEnergy losses\n• heating of the copper coils (I²R)\n• eddy currents in the core heat the iron\n• energy needed to magnetise and demagnetise the core each cycle (hysteresis)\n• some flux leakage, so not all primary flux links the secondary\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A hospital uses technetium-99m, a gamma emitter with a half-life of 6 hours, as a tracer injected into patients for imaging. A doctor is asked why an alpha emitter is not used instead. Hospital staff prepare and inject many doses each day.\n\nEvaluate the choice of a gamma emitter for this use, with reference to the properties of the radiations, the absorbed and equivalent doses received by the patient, and the risks to the staff and how they are reduced.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nProperties of radiations\n• alpha is strongly ionising and has a short range (a few cm in air, less than 0.1 mm in tissue), so it would not leave the body to reach the camera\n• gamma is weakly ionising and penetrating, so it escapes the body and can be detected outside\n• a 6 hour half-life is long enough for imaging but the activity soon falls\nDoses and risk to the patient\n• absorbed dose = energy absorbed / mass (Gy)\n• equivalent dose = absorbed dose × radiation weighting factor (Sv); alpha factor 20, gamma 1\n• an alpha emitter inside the body would deposit all its energy in a small mass of tissue, giving a large equivalent dose\n• risk of cancer is roughly proportional to equivalent dose, so gamma gives a lower risk; benefit of diagnosis outweighs the small risk\nRisks to staff and precautions\n• staff are exposed repeatedly, outside the body, so gamma is the main hazard\n• increase distance (inverse square law), reduce time, use shielding (lead syringe shields, lead glass)\n• wear monitoring badges to record dose; keep doses below legal limits\n• avoid contamination (gloves) to prevent ingestion\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` }
    ]
  }
};

if (typeof module !== 'undefined') module.exports = PHYSICS_OCR_B_PRACTICE;
